var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#Time-response","page":"Time response","text":"This package calculates all scattering in the frequency domain, and we call the resulting field the frequency response hat u(mathbf xomega), which satisfies nabla^2 hat u(mathbf xomega) + k^2 hat u(mathbf xomega) = 0, where k = dfracomegac. We can transform the frequency response hat u(mathbf xomega) into a time response u(mathbf xt) using a Fourier transform, where u(mathbf xt) satisfies nabla^2 u(mathbf xt) - frac1c^2  fracpartial^2partial t^2u(mathbf xt) = 0. For a minimal example see Results in time, or see Technical details for more maths.\n\nnote: Note\nThe package assumes the time response u(mathbf xt) is always real, this simplifies the Fourier transform.","title":"Time response"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#impulse_intro","page":"Time response","text":"As an example, let use a plane-wave source mathrm e^mathrm i omega x and measure the response at origin of the source x = (00),\n\nusing MultipleScattering;\n\nplane_wave = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2); direction = [1.0, 0.0], position = [0.0, 0.0]);\nx = [[0.0, 0.0]];\nωs = LinRange(0.0, 1.0, 100);\nfreq_response = run(plane_wave, x, ωs);\nt_vec = LinRange(-20.0, 80.0, 110);\ntime_response = frequency_to_time(freq_response; t_vec = t_vec);\n\nwhere we specified the times t_vec to calculate time_response. If no t_vec is given, the default times would be t_vec = ω_to_t(ωs) which is the standard choice for the Discrete Fourier Transform.  \n\nLet us have a look at these responses:\n\nusing Plots;\n\np1 = plot(freq_response, xlims = (0, 2), ylims = (0.0, 1.5), field_apply = real);\np2 = plot(time_response);\nplot(p1, p2)\n\n(Image: A discrete delta impulse)\n\nNote how the time response has lobes after and before the largest signal. This is often undesirable, as we usually want signal which are compact in the time domain, i.e. zero before and after the largest signal. These lobes are called Gibbs Phenomena. They are caused by only calculating the frequency response hat u(mathbf xomega) up to omega leq 1, and then (usually), taking hat u(mathbf xomega) = 0 for omega  1 when calculating the Fourier transform.   \n\nWe can alter the response, in time and frequency, by specifying an impulse function hat f(omega) which will use hat phi(mathbf x omega) = hat f(omega) u(mathbf x omega) as the frequency response. For example, we can choose hat f(omega) to smooth out the drastic drop in hat u(mathbf 0omega) when omega passes over omega = 1.","title":"Intro"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#Gaussian-impulse","page":"Time response","text":"The simplest way to avoid unwanted lobes (and Gibbs phenomena) is to use a Gaussian impulse function:\n\nmaxω = maximum(ωs);\ngauss_impulse = GaussianImpulse(maxω);\ntypeof(gauss_impulse)\n\n# output\nContinuousImpulse{Float64}\n\nThe argument maxω passed to GaussianImpulse will return an Gaussian impulse which will (mostly) avoid the lobes given by calculating only ω <= maxω. The Gaussian impulse in frequency and time is\n\nωs_all = -2.0:0.01:2.0;\np1 = plot(ω -> real(gauss_impulse.in_freq(ω)), ωs_all, title=\"Gaussian in frequency\");\np2 = plot(gauss_impulse.in_time, t_vec, title=\"Gaussian in time\");\nplot(p1, p2)\n\n(Image: A Gaussian impulse)\n\nThe analytic expression for these functions are hat f(omega) = 2 sqrt3 pi  Omega^2 mathrm e^-3 (omegaOmega)^2 and f(t) = mathrm e^-(t Omega)^2  12, where we used Omega =  mathrmmaxomega.\n\nTo use this impulse we simply:\n\ngauss_time_response = frequency_to_time(freq_response; t_vec = t_vec, impulse = gauss_impulse);\np1 = plot(time_response);\np2 = plot(gauss_time_response);\nplot(p1, p2)\n\n(Image: Compare the Gaussian impulse)\n\nThere are still some lobes present because again freq_response only calculates ω<=1.0,  but this time the drop is much less pronounced, which we can demonstrate with a plot of hat phi(mathbf 0 omega):  \n\nφs = field(freq_response)[:] .* gauss_impulse.in_freq.(ωs);\nplot(ωs, real.(φs), title=\"Frequency response φ\")\n\n(Image: Compare the Gaussian impulse)","title":"Gaussian impulse"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#Discrete-impulse","page":"Time response","text":"The only impulse the package provides is the Gaussian, both its discrete DiscreteGaussianImpulse and analytic form GaussianImpulse. But all this is not necessary to use your own defined impulse function. You only need to define an impulse sampled in frequency. For example suppose we want a triangle impulse in frequency:\n\n# we need only define for ω > 0.0\ntriangle_freq(ω) = 5 - 5*ω;\n\n# we only need the sampled frequency response.\nin_freq = triangle_freq.(ωs);\n\n# as we have specified in_freq we do not need to specify in_time.\nin_time = 0.0*t_vec;\n\ndiscrete_impulse = DiscreteImpulse(t_vec, in_time, ωs, in_freq);\n\ntime_response = frequency_to_time(freq_response; t_vec = t_vec, discrete_impulse = discrete_impulse);\n\nplot(time_response)\n\n(Image: )\n\nAlternatively, we can attempt to produce a triangle wave in the time domain, for which there is a convenient constructor:\n\ntriangle_time(t) = (abs(t/15) < 1) ? 1 - abs(t/15) : 0.0;\n\nin_time = triangle_time.(t_vec);\n\n# the function DiscreteImpulse below will calculate in_freq\ndiscrete_impulse = DiscreteImpulse(t_vec, in_time, ωs);\n\ntime_response = frequency_to_time(freq_response; t_vec = t_vec, discrete_impulse = discrete_impulse);\n\nplot!(time_response)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Discrete impulse"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#lens_example","page":"Time response","text":"As an example, we will make a reflective lens out of particles. To achieve this we will place the particles into a region with the shape TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint.\n\nFirst we choose the properties of the lens:\n\np_radius = 0.1;\nvolfrac = 0.3;\n\nx = [-10.0; 0.0];\noutertime = 34.8;\ninnertime = 34.0;\n\n# Generate particles which are at most outertime away from our listener\noutershape = TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint(x, outertime)\nouterparticles = random_particles(Acoustic(2; ρ=0.0, c=0.0), Circle(p_radius);\n        region_shape = outershape,\n        volume_fraction = volfrac,\n        seed=2\n);\n\n# Filter out particles which are less than innertime away\ninnershape = TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint(x, innertime + 4*p_radius); # the + 4*p_radius is to account for double the particle diameter\nparticles = filter(p -> p⊈innershape, outerparticles);\n\nplot(particles)\n\n(Image: )\n\nNext we simulate an impulse plane-wave starting at x = -10:\n\nωs = LinRange(0.01, 2.0, 100);\n\nplane_wave = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2); direction = [1.0, 0.0], position = x);\nsim = FrequencySimulation(particles, plane_wave);\n\nfreq_response = run(sim, x, ωs);\n\nt_vec = -10.:0.2:81.\ntime_response = frequency_to_time(freq_response; t_vec=t_vec, impulse = GaussianImpulse(1.5; σ = 1.0));\n\nxticks = [0.0, 20.0, 34.0, 40.0, 60.0, 80.0];\nplot(time_response, title=\"Time response from lens\", label=\"\", xticks=xticks)\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe first peak is the incident wave, and the next peak is the wave scattered from the lens which should arrive close to t = 34.","title":"Lens example"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/time_response/#impulse_details","page":"Time response","text":"We can calculate the time response u(mathbf xt), from the frequency response hat u(mathbf x omega) by approximating the Fourier transform:\n\nu(mathbf xt) = frac12pi int_-infty^infty hat u(mathbf x omega)mathrm e^-mathrm i omega t domega\nquad hat u(mathbf x omega) = int_-infty^infty u(mathbf x t)mathrm e^mathrm i omega t dt\n\nwhere the second equation is the inverse transform. To modify the time response u, we can specify an impulse function hat f(omega) which gives an new time response function phi(mathbf x t):\n\nphi(mathbf x t) = frac12pi int_-infty^infty hat f(omega) hat u(mathbf x omega)mathrm e^-mathrm i omega t domega = frac1pimathrmRe int_0^Omega hat f(omega) hat u(mathbf x omega)mathrm e^-mathrm i omega t domega\n\nwhere the second identity results from assuming that phi(mathbf x t) is real, with mathrm Re being the real part. Also note that phi(mathbf xt) = (f * u)(mathbf x t), where * is a convolution in time.\n\nTo approximate the above integral as finite integral, one option is to assume that hat f(omega) hat u(mathbf x omega) to 0 as omega to infty, which would allow us to truncate the integration domain between omega in -Omega Omega. We also need to discretise the integral. Putting both of these together results in\n\nphi(mathbf x t) approx frac1pimathrmRe sum_m=0^M hat f(omega_m) hat u(mathbf x omega_m)mathrm e^-mathrm i omega_m t Delta omega_m\n\nwhere omega_M = Omega and Delta omega_m depends on the scheme used, with the simplest being Delta omega_m = omega_m+1 - omega_m.\n\nTo learn more see the notes Discrete Fourier Transform or the tests in the folder test of the source code.\n\ntip: Tip\nThe standard way to sample the frequencies is to take omega_m = m Delta omega and Delta omega_m = Delta omega for some constant Delta omega. If we substitute this sampling into the approximation for phi(mathbf x t), shown above, we find that phi(mathbf xt) becomes periodic in time t with period T = 2pi  Delta omega. That is phi(mathbf x t + T) = phi(mathbf x t) for every t. Suppose you were calculating a scattered wave that arrives at time t = T + tau, what would happen? The answer is you would see this scattered wave arrive at time t = tau, assuming tau  T. This wrong arrival time occurs often when waves get trapped due to strong multiple scattering.","title":"Technical details"},{"category":"section","location":"example/hankel_convergence/README/#Convergence-when-increasing-the-number-of-Hankel-functions","page":"Convergence when increasing the number of Hankel functions","text":"The code convergence.jl tests how fast does the scattered wave (in frequency) converge when increasing the number of Hankel functions. To describe the scattered wave from each particle we use a series of Hankel functions (of the first kind).\n\ninclude(\"convergence.jl\")\nsimulations = hankel_order_convergence()\nplot_hankel_order_convergence(simulations)\n\n\n(Image: Plot lens shape and response in time)\n\nIn the figures above m is the maximum order of the Hankel functions. The top left figure shows the configuration of particles considered.  ","title":"Convergence when increasing the number of Hankel functions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/shapes/#Shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"Shape is an abstract type which represents the shape of particles, and also the regions where particles are placed or where fields are calculated. See Shape for a list of relevant types and functions.","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/shapes/#Existing-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"Shape Description\nSphere A circle in 2D and a sphere in 3D. Circle(radius) and Sphere(radius) are short for a circle or sphere at the origin.\nBox A rectangle in 2D and a box in 3D, with sides parallel to the axes. Rectangle(bottomleft, topright) also creates a 2D box.\nHalfspace All points behind a plane, defined by its outward normal and a point on the plane.\nPlate All points between two parallel planes.\nPolygon A 2D polygon defined by its vertices.\nIsotropicHelmholtz, Helmholtz 2D Helmholtz resonators: a circular wall with an opening (aperture).\nTimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint, TimeOfFlightPointWaveToPoint Regions which contain all particles that can be reached by a wave, and then scatter back to a listener, within a given time.\nEmptyShape A shape with no points.\n\nFor example:\n\njulia> circle = Sphere([-1.0, 0.0], 1.0) # origin and radius\nSphere{Float64, 2}([-1.0, 0.0], 1.0)\n\njulia> rectangle = Box([[0.0, -1.0], [1.0, 2.0]]) # the smallest box which contains these points\nBox{Float64, 2}([0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 3.0])\n\njulia> name(circle), name(rectangle)\n(\"Circle\", \"Rectangle\")\n\nYou can plot shapes by using:\n\nusing Plots;\n\ntimeofflight = TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint([1.0, 0.0], 3.0)\n\nplot(rectangle, linecolor = :red)\nplot!(circle, linecolor = :green)\nplot!(timeofflight, linecolor = :blue)\n\n(Image: Plot the three shapes)\n\nThe Box and TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint are usually regions where particles are placed. The time of flight shape contains all particles, from a halfspace, which can be reached by a plane wave and then scatter back to the listener at most a time t later. The Sphere is also used to define circular or spherical particles.","title":"Existing shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/shapes/#Working-with-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"Check if a point is inside a shape, or if a shape is inside another shape:\n\njulia> [0.5, 0.5] ∈ rectangle\ntrue\n\njulia> Sphere([0.5, 0.5], 0.4) ⊆ rectangle\ntrue\n\njulia> circle ⊆ rectangle\nfalse\n\nCalculate the size of shapes:\n\njulia> volume(rectangle) # the area in 2D\n3.0\n\njulia> outer_radius(rectangle) # the radius of the smallest circle, around the origin, which contains the shape\n1.5811388300841898\n\njulia> bounding_box(circle) # the smallest box which contains the shape\nBox{Float64, 2}([-1.0, 0.0], [2.0, 2.0])\n\nShapes which are the same, except for their origin, are congruent. Particles which are congruent have the same T-matrix, which saves time in simulations.\n\njulia> circle2 = congruent(circle, [4.0, 4.0]) # the same shape but with origin [4.0, 4.0]\nSphere{Float64, 2}([4.0, 4.0], 1.0)\n\njulia> iscongruent(circle, circle2) # can also be written as circle ≅ circle2\ntrue\n\nOther useful functions include boundary_points, which returns points on the boundary of a shape, and points_in_shape, which returns a grid of points inside a shape.","title":"Working with shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/shapes/#New-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"To define a new shape see New particles.","title":"New shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"example/box_size/README/#Box-size","page":"Box size","text":"If we are only interested in the response at a specific location for a certain time interval, we need only simulate particles which are this distance (or time) away from the listener.","title":"Box size"},{"category":"section","location":"example/helmholtz-resonator/resonator/#Helmholtz-resonator","page":"Helmholtz resonator","text":"\n\nThe code Resonator.jl simulates a 2D Helmlholtz resonator by aligning particles in the shape of a bottle.\n\nA Helmholtz resonator is made of a cavity with a small opening mouth. When illuminated with an acoustic waves at its specific resonant frequencies, the scattered field inside the cavity reaches high amplitudes. Those frequencies of course depend on the dimensions of the resonator that we set below.\n\nusing MultipleScattering\nusing Plots\n\n# The four parameters below are all the required dimensions of the resonator.\ncavity_radius = 1.0;\ncavity_length = 2.5;\nmouth_radius = 0.3;\nmouth_length = 0.5;\n\nTo simulate the resonator, we align small circular particles to form a cavity. In the code below, the positions of the particles are stored in the vector X.\n\nradius = .1; # radius of the particles\nd_particle = 2.001*radius; # distance between centers\n# define the cavity of the resonator\ncavity_up = [\n    [x,cavity_radius+radius]\nfor x = radius:d_particle:cavity_length];\ncavity_down = [\n    [x,-cavity_radius-radius]\nfor x = radius:d_particle:cavity_length]\n\ncavity_right = [\n    [cavity_length+.05*radius,y]\nfor y = (- cavity_radius - radius):d_particle:(cavity_radius+2*radius)]\n\n# define the mouth of the resonator\nmouth_connect_down = [\n    [radius-d_particle,y]\nfor y=(-cavity_radius-radius):d_particle:(-mouth_radius)]\nmouth_connect_up = [\n    [radius-d_particle,y]\nfor y=(mouth_radius+radius):d_particle:(cavity_radius+2*radius)]\n\nmouth_up = [\n    [x,mouth_radius+radius]\nfor x = radius-2*d_particle:-d_particle:-mouth_length-radius]\nmouth_down = [\n    [x,-mouth_radius-radius]\nfor x = radius-2*d_particle:-d_particle:-mouth_length-radius]\n\n# put the different pieces together\nX = [cavity_up; cavity_down; cavity_right; mouth_connect_down; mouth_connect_up; mouth_up; mouth_down];\nX = [x - [cavity_length/2,cavity_radius/2-0.5] for x in X];\n\nNow we can define the resonator:\n\nparticle_medium =  Acoustic(2; ρ = 0., c = 0.);\nResonator = [\n    Particle(particle_medium, Circle(x, radius))\nfor x in X];\nplot(Resonator)\nsavefig(\"Resonator.png\")\n\n(Image: Resonator)\n\nNext we calculate the scattered field for an incoming plane wave at different frequencies.\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ=1.0, c=1.0); # medium of the background, 2 is the dimension of the setting.\nsource = plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0,0.0])\n\n# region where the result will be plot\nM=N=5.0;\nbottomleft = [-M;-N]; topright = [M;N];\nregion = Box([bottomleft, topright]);\n\nsim = FrequencySimulation(Resonator, source);\nlist_ω = [1.99,3.99,2.74]\nresult = run(sim, region, list_ω, basis_order=5, only_scattered_waves = true; res=200)\n\nWe plot the results for the different frequencies and observe that omega_0=199 and omega_1=399 correspond to resonant frequencies.\n\nfor p=1:3 # loop on the different frequencies\n    plot(result, list_ω[p]; seriestype = :heatmap) # clim=(-5.0,5.0)\n    colormap(\"RdBu\")\n    plot!(Resonator,colorbar=true,\n       title=\"Field at ω=\"*string(list_ω[p]),axis=false, xguide =\"\", yguide =\"\")\n    savefig(\"plot_\"*string(p)*\".png\")\nend\n\n\nThe following plots are obtained:\n\n(Image: p1) (Image: p3) (Image: p2)","title":"Helmholtz resonator"},{"category":"section","location":"example/two_particles/README/#Two-particles","page":"Two particles","text":"Define two particles with the first centred at [1.0,-4.0], with radius 1.0, sound speed 20.0, and density 10.0. The second particle is centered at [3.0,3.0], with radius 3.0, sound speed 1.0, and density 0.1.\n\nusing MultipleScattering\nusing Plots\npyplot()\n\np1 = Particle(Acoustic(2; c = 20.0, ρ = 10.0),Sphere([1.0,-4.0], 1.0))\np2 = Particle(Acoustic(2; c = 1.0, ρ = 0.1),Sphere([3.0,3.0], 3.0))\nparticles = [p1,p2]\n\nSpecify the angular frequency of the incident wave and calculate the response.\n\nωs = collect(0.1:0.01:1.0)\nsource = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2));\n# Calculate and plot the frequency response at x.\nx = [[-10.0,0.0]];\nsimulation = run(particles, source, x, ωs)\nplot(simulation)\n\n(Image: Plot against frequency)\n\nThe above used an incident plane with the default position at [0.0, 0.0] and was simultated in the x direction. To change these defaults use:\n\nx = [[-10.0,-10.0]]\nsource = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2); direction = [1.0,1.0], position = [0.0,0.0]);\nsimulation = run(particles, source, x, ωs)\n\nThen plot the response around the particles and receiver.\n\nregion = Box([[-11.0;-11.0], [6.0;6.0]])\nω = 3.2\nresult = run(particles, source, region, [ω]; res=80)\nplot(result, ω; field_apply=abs, seriestype = :contour)\n\n(Image: Plot absolute value of wave field)\n\nThe green circle in the plot is the receiver position. Looking at the region between the particles we see the complicated results of multiple scatttering.","title":"Two particles"},{"category":"section","location":"example/intro/README/#[Go-to-introductory-example](https://github.com/JuliaWaveScattering/MultipleScattering.jl).","page":"Go to introductory example.","text":"","title":"Go to introductory example."},{"category":"section","location":"maths/README/#Theory","page":"Theory","text":"Below are papers and notes that explain the theory behind this package.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"maths/README/#[The-equations-of-Multiple-Scattering](multiplescattering.pdf)","page":"Theory","text":"These short notes introduce the T-matrix, which defines how a particle scatters waves, and shows how it used in multiple scattering from many particles.","title":"The equations of Multiple Scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"maths/README/#[Multiple-Scattering-in-the-context-of-acoustics](acoustics.pdf)","page":"Theory","text":"These notes present the T-matrix construction in the context of acoustics, giving insight into the previous notes. ","title":"Multiple Scattering in the context of acoustics"},{"category":"section","location":"maths/README/#[Discrete-Fourier-Transform](DiscreteFourier.pdf)","page":"Theory","text":"These notes give details on Green’s functions and Fourier transforms. We mostly focus on using discrete impulse functions.","title":"Discrete Fourier Transform"},{"category":"section","location":"maths/README/#[T-matrix-Software](a9-ganesh.pdf)","page":"Theory","text":"This paper describes a method to calculate the T-matrix for new particles.","title":"T-matrix Software"},{"category":"section","location":"example/near_surface_backscattering/README/#Near-surface-backscattering","page":"Near-surface backscattering","text":"Near-surface backscattering is a method of accurately calculating the backscattering from an infinite halfspace. For just the code see backscattering.jl First, let us see why it is difficult to approximate the scattering from a halfspace filled with particles. That is, let us find out how many particles are required before the backscattering converges.","title":"Near-surface backscattering"},{"category":"section","location":"example/near_surface_backscattering/README/#Generate-a-large-material-filled-with-particles.","page":"Near-surface backscattering","text":"using MultipleScattering\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2)\n\nradius = 0.8\nvolfrac = 0.10\nmax_width = 70.\n\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(0.2, 0.1, 2)\nparticle_shape = Circle(radius)\n\nbottomleft = [0.,-max_width]\ntopright = [max_width,max_width]\n\nshape = Box([bottomleft,topright])\nparticles = random_particles(particle_medium, particle_shape; region_shape = shape, volume_fraction = volfrac)\n\nWe send an incoming harmonic plane wave and receive the backscattering at x:\n\nusing Plots\npyplot(linewidth=2)\n\nx = [-10.,0.]\nsource =  plane_source(host_medium; position = x,\n        direction = [1.0,0.],\n        amplitude = 1.0)\n\nplot(particles)\nscatter!([x[1]],[x[2]], lab=\"\")\nannotate!([(x[1], x[2] -max_width/10., \"Receiver\")])\nplot!(shape, linecolor = :red)\n\n(Image: The largest quantity of particles used)","title":"Generate a large material filled with particles."},{"category":"section","location":"example/near_surface_backscattering/README/#Calculate-backscattering-for-different-quantity-of-particles","page":"Near-surface backscattering","text":"We will shave off particles on the right of this group of particles (above), and then calculate the backscattered waves for a range of angular frequencies ωs.\n\nωs = collect(0.01:0.01:1.)\nwidths = 10.:5.:max_width\nnum_particles = zeros(length(widths))\n\nresults = map(eachindex(widths)) do i\n    bottomleft = [0.,-widths[i]]\n    topright = [widths[i],widths[i]]\n    shape = Box([bottomleft, topright])\n\n    ps = filter(p -> p ⊆ shape, particles) # select particles that are inside shape\n    num_particles[i] = Int(length(ps))\n\n    simulation = FrequencySimulation(ps, source)\n    run(simulation, x, ωs)\nend\n\nbackscattered_waves = field.(results)\n\nM = length(backscattered_waves)\nbM = backscattered_waves[M] # backscattering from largest material\ndifferences = [norm(b - bM) for b in backscattered_waves[1:(M-1)]]./norm(bM)\n\nplot_converge = plot(num_particles[1:(M-1)], differences,\n    xlabel = \"number of particles\", yguide =\"error %\",\n    label=\"frequency convergence\"\n)\n\n(Image: The convergence of the response in frequency, when increasing the number of particles)\n\nThe graph shows the rate of convergence, that is, how much the backscattering changes when including more particles (making the material deeper). The graph has not clearly converged, so we can only conclude that more than 400 particles are needed to accurately approximate the backscattering from an infinite halfspace.\n\nWe can accelerate this convergence by considering backscattering in time.","title":"Calculate backscattering for different quantity of particles"},{"category":"section","location":"example/near_surface_backscattering/README/#Calculate-backscattering-in-time","page":"Near-surface backscattering","text":"time_simulations = frequency_to_time.(results)\nreceiver = results[1].x[1]\ntimes = 2*(widths .- receiver[1]) # time it takes for an incident plane wave to reach the furthest particles and then return to the receiver\n\nplot()\nfor i in [1,3,6,9,12,13]\n    plot!(time_simulations[i],label=\"$(num_particles[i]) particles\"\n        , xlims=(0,maximum(times)+10.), ylims=(-0.2,0.1)\n        , xticks = [0.; 30.; times]\n    )\nend\ngui()\n\n(Image: The responses in time for different quantity of particles)\n\nWe see that the responses in time diverge from each other more and more as time goes by. Meaning that if we only calculate the response for a short amount of time 34, then the convergence will be accelerated.\n\ntime_vec = 0.:pi:34.2\ntime_results = frequency_to_time.(results; t_vec = time_vec, impulse = GaussianImpulse(maximum(ωs)))\n\nbackscattered_waves = field.(time_results)\nbM = backscattered_waves[M] # backscattering from largest material\ndifferences = [norm(b - bM) for b in backscattered_waves[1:(M-1)]]./norm(bM)\nplot(plot_converge)\nplot!(num_particles[1:(M-1)], differences, xlabel = \"number of particles\", yguide =\"error %\", label=\"time convergence\")\n\n(Image: Compare converges for responses in time and responses in frequency)\n\nThe convergence of the time response, for time 0<t<34, is much faster. In fact, less than 100 particles are needed to accurately approximate the backscattering from an infinite halfspace. The reason we don't show these as log plots is because there is a small constant error (about 0.01%) due to the discrete Fourier transform. This error is caused by the Gibbs phenomena and by assuming the backscattering is periodic (when it is not). Both these errors are well understood and can be controlled.","title":"Calculate backscattering in time"},{"category":"section","location":"example/near_surface_backscattering/README/#Calculate-backscattering-only-from-near-surface-particles","page":"Near-surface backscattering","text":"This last step is about efficiency. We want to only include particle which contribute to the backscattering for short time intervals. To do this we created a region called TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint(listener,time), where every particle in this shape takes less than time for their first scattered wave (due to an incident plane wave) to return to the listener.  More precisely, if listener = (lx,ly), then every point (x,y) inside this shape satisfies: x-lx+((x-lx)^2+(y-ly)^2)^(1/2)<time and x>0.\n\nFor example, look at the largest quantity of particle we used\n\nlistener_position = [-10.,0.]\nshape = TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint(listener_position,80.0)\nscatter([listener_position[1]],[listener_position[2]]);\nannotate!([(listener_position[1], listener_position[2] -max_width/10., \"Receiver\")])\nplot!.(particles);\nplot!(shape)\n\n(Image: Shows the particles in the shape TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint)\n\nFor time 0<t<80 the backscattering from these particles is the same as an infinite halfspace filled with particles. To achieve this result we need only the particles inside the shape TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint (region with the red outline). The particles outside this shape were unnecessary. To see this inaction:\n\ntimes = 40.:15.:80.\nnear_surface_simulations = map(times) do t\n    shape = TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint(receiver,t) # choose a material with particles only in the near surface region\n    ps = filter(p -> p ⊆ shape, particles) # select particles that are inside shape\n    run(FrequencySimulation(ps, source), x, ωs) # calculate backscattering\nend\n\ntime_near_simulations = frequency_to_time.(near_surface_simulations; impulse = GaussianImpulse(maximum(ωs)))\n\nplot()\nfor i in 1:length(times)\n    plot!(time_near_simulations[i],label=\"time of flight $(times[i])\"\n        , xlims=(0,maximum(times)+10.), ylims=(-0.6,0.3)\n        , xticks = [0.; times], title=\"\"\n    )\nend\ngui()\n\n(Image: Response from particles in the shapes TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint)\n\nNote the incident pulse has a thickness of about 10 in time, which is why the time of flight 40 diverges from the other curves slightly before time 40, and likewise for the other curves.","title":"Calculate backscattering only from near-surface particles"},{"category":"section","location":"example/particles_in_circle/README/#Random-particles-in-a-circle","page":"Random particles in a circle","text":"The code particles_in_circle.jl compares the scattered wave from one big circle, with the scattered wave from a circle filled with small particles.\n\nusing MultipleScattering\n\n#You can also pick your own shape, an generate random particles inside it\n#with a certain radius ands volume fraction\nradius = 0.3\nvolfrac = 0.45\ncentre = [0.,0.]\nbig_radius = 3.0\n\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ=0.0, c=0.0) # 2D particle with density ρ = 0.0 and soundspeed c = 0.0\nparticle_shape = Circle(radius)\n\ncircle = Sphere(centre, big_radius)\n\nparticles = random_particles(particle_medium, particle_shape; region_shape = circle, volume_fraction = volfrac, seed=1)\n\nx = [-10.,0.] # position to receive the reflected wave\nhost_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ=1.0, c=1.0)\nsource =  plane_source(host_medium; position = x, direction = [1.0,0.])\nsimulation = FrequencySimulation(particles, source)\n\nThe particles chosen are impenetrable, i.e. the wave is 100\\% reflected. So this circle filled with scatterers should act like one big particle.\n\nbig_particle = Particle(particle_medium, circle)\nbig_particle_simulation = FrequencySimulation([big_particle], source)\n\n#define a bounding box for plot\n    bottomleft = [-10, -2*big_radius]\n    topright = [big_radius, 2*big_radius]\n    box = Box([bottomleft, topright])\n\nusing Plots\nheight = 300\n#gr(size=(1.4*height,height))\n#pyplot(leg=false, size=(1.4*height,height))\n\nω = 0.5\n#plot(big_particle_simulation, ω; res=15, bounds = box);\n#plot!(big_particle)\n\n#savefig(\"plot_field_big.png\")\n\n#plot(simulation, ω; res=15, bounds = box);\n#plot!(particles, linecolor = :green)\n\n#savefig(\"plot_field.png\")\n\nResulting in the figures:\n\n(Image: The field with big particle) (Image: The field with particles)\n\nIf we compare the response measured at the listener [-10., 0.], they should be very similar:\n\n#define angular frequency range\nωs = collect(LinRange(0.1,1.0,10))\nresult = run(simulation, x, ωs)\nbig_result = run(big_particle_simulation, x, ωs)\n\n#plot(result, lab = \"scattering from particles\")\n#plot!(big_result,\n#lab = \"scattering from big particle\",\n#title=\"Compare scattered wave from one big particle, \\n and a circle filled with small particles\")\n\n(Image: The response comparison)","title":"Random particles in a circle"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Simulations-and-results","page":"Simulations and results","text":"This page explains what happens when you run a FrequencySimulation, which keyword arguments control the accuracy, and how to use the results. For a first example see the Introduction.","title":"Simulations and results"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Setting-up-a-simulation","page":"Simulations and results","text":"A FrequencySimulation needs a vector of particles and a source, where the medium of the source is the background (host) medium:\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0)\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 0.5, c = 0.6)\n\nparticles = [\n    Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([0.0, 0.0], 1.0)),\n    Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([3.0, 1.0], 0.5))\n]\n\nsource = plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0, 0.0])\nsim = FrequencySimulation(particles, source)\n\n# output\n\nFrequencySimulation{2}\nmedium    = Acoustic(1.0, 1.0 + 0.0im, 2)\nparticles = \nParticle(Acoustic(0.5, 0.6 + 0.0im, 2), Sphere{Float64, 2}([0.0, 0.0], 1.0))\nParticle(Acoustic(0.5, 0.6 + 0.0im, 2), Sphere{Float64, 2}([3.0, 1.0], 0.5))\n\nA simulation with no particles, FrequencySimulation(source), is also allowed and just returns the source field. Particles are not allowed to overlap; overlapping_pairs returns the pairs of particles which do.","title":"Setting up a simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#run_maths","page":"Simulations and results","text":"Let mathbf x_j be the origin of particle j, k = omega  c the wavenumber of the host medium, and let mathrm v_n and mathrm u_n be the regular and outgoing basis functions, given by regular_basis_function and outgoing_basis_function. In 2D\n\nmathrm v_n(mathbf x) = mathrm J_n(k r) mathrm e^mathrm i n theta qquad mathrm u_n(mathbf x) = mathrm H^(1)_n(k r) mathrm e^mathrm i n theta\n\nwhere (r theta) are polar coordinates, and in 3D mathrm j_ell(k r) mathrm Y_ell^m(hatmathbf x) and mathrm h^(1)_ell(k r) mathrm Y_ell^m(hatmathbf x), with spherical Bessel and Hankel functions and spherical harmonics.\n\nOutside of the particles, the total field is the source field plus the waves scattered by each particle:\n\nu(mathbf x) = u_textin(mathbf x) + sum_j sum_n f^j_n  mathrm u_n(mathbf x - mathbf x_j)\n\nTo find the scattering coefficients f^j_n, run does the following:\n\nCalculates the T-matrix T^j of each particle with t_matrix. This matrix gives the waves scattered by a particle in terms of the waves incident on it. Congruent particles share the same T-matrix, see get_t_matrices.\nExpands the source around each particle, u_textin(mathbf x) = sum_n a^j_n mathrm v_n(mathbf x - mathbf x_j), where the vector of coefficients a^j is given by source.coefficients(basis_order, x_j, ω).\nSolves the multiple scattering system: the wave incident on particle j is the source plus the waves scattered by all other particles,\nf^j = T^j g^j qquad g^j = a^j + sum_l not = j U(mathbf x_j - mathbf x_l)^mathsf T f^l\nwhere U is given by outgoing_translation_matrix. This is the linear system built with scattering_matrix and solved in basis_coefficients.\nEvaluates the field at the requested points. Inside a particle it uses internal_field instead.\n\nThe coefficients f^j_n are returned by basis_coefficients, with one column for each particle:\n\njulia> ω = 2.0;\n\njulia> f = basis_coefficients(sim, ω; basis_order = 4);\n\njulia> size(f) # 2*4 + 1 = 9 coefficients in 2D for each particle\n(9, 2)\n\nFor the details of this method see the notes in Theory.","title":"What run calculates"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Choosing-the-basis-order","page":"Simulations and results","text":"The sums over n above are truncated at the basis order N, using n leq N in 2D and ell leq N in 3D. This is the most important parameter for the accuracy and the speed of a simulation. The default is basis_order = 5.\n\nA larger basis order is needed for larger particles or higher frequencies. A good first guess is given by estimate_outgoing_basisorder, which checks the convergence of the T-matrix of a particle:\n\njulia> estimate_outgoing_basisorder(host_medium, particles[1], ω)\n4\n\nWhen particles are close together the waves between them vary more rapidly, so it is good practice to check that the results do not change when increasing the basis order:\n\njulia> x = [5.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> us = [field(run(sim, x, ω; basis_order = N))[1] for N in [2, 4, 8, 12]];\n\njulia> round.(abs.(us[1:3] .- us[4]), sigdigits = 2) # difference from using basis_order = 12\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.0038\n 0.00012\n 4.3e-11","title":"Choosing the basis order"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Running-for-many-positions-and-frequencies","page":"Simulations and results","text":"The positions can be one vector or a vector of vectors, and the angular frequencies can be one number or a vector:\n\njulia> result = run(sim, [[5.0, 0.0], [0.0, 3.0]], [1.0, 2.0]);\n\njulia> size(result) # (number of positions, number of frequencies)\n(2, 2)\n\njulia> round.(field(result), digits = 4)\n2×2 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n 0.6596-0.4263im  0.2426-0.681im\n 0.9771-0.1341im  1.1626-0.5013im\n\nYou can also skip creating a FrequencySimulation and use run(particles, source, x, ωs).\n\nWhen running for many frequencies, the keyword basis_order is used for the largest frequency. To save time, smaller basis orders can be used for smaller frequencies with min_basis_order. Then the basis order increases linearly from min_basis_order, for the smallest frequency, up to basis_order, for the largest. Alternatively, give one basis order for each frequency with basis_order_vec.\n\nIf the first frequency is zero, then the field for ω = 0 is extrapolated from the next two frequencies, as the T-matrices are often not defined at ω = 0. This is useful when converting to the Time response.","title":"Running for many positions and frequencies"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Running-over-a-region","page":"Simulations and results","text":"To calculate the field over a region, pass a Shape instead of positions:\n\njulia> region = Box([[-2.0, -2.0], [5.0, 3.0]]);\n\njulia> result = run(sim, region, ω; res = 10);\n\njulia> size(result) # an 11 x 11 grid of points\n(121, 1)\n\nThe grid of points covers the bounding_box of the region, and the field is set to zero for points which are not inside the region. Use xres and yres to choose the resolution along each axis, and exclude_region to avoid evaluating the field in some shape, for example close to a point source. In 3D the field is calculated on the x-z plane at the height y, which defaults to y = 0, using xres and zres. See points_in_shape for details.","title":"Running over a region"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Only-the-scattered-waves","page":"Simulations and results","text":"Use only_scattered_waves = true to remove the source from the field outside of the particles:\n\njulia> x_out = [5.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> u_total = field(run(sim, x_out, ω; basis_order = 8))[1];\n\njulia> u_scat = field(run(sim, x_out, ω; basis_order = 8, only_scattered_waves = true))[1];\n\njulia> u_total ≈ u_scat + field(source, x_out, ω)\ntrue\n\nInside the particles the total field is always returned.","title":"Only the scattered waves"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation/#Results","page":"Simulations and results","text":"run returns a FrequencySimulationResult, with the fields:\n\nresult.x: the positions, a vector of SVector;\nresult.ω: the angular frequencies;\nresult.field: a matrix of SVector with one row for each position and one column for each frequency.\n\nFor scalar waves, such as acoustics, field returns a matrix of complex numbers instead, and field(result, i, j) returns the field at position i and frequency j. Results can be added together, or multiplied by numbers, for example 2.0 * result1 + result2.\n\nTo convert the results to the time domain, use frequency_to_time, see Time response. To plot the results see Plotting.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/particles/#Particles","page":"Particles","text":"Particle is a struct which define a scatterer, obstacle, or simply particle, which can scatter waves. See Particle for a list of relevant types and functions.\n\nA Particle have two fields: medium and shape. The medium defines what kind of waves can propagate inside Particle, and what type of RegularSource can be used to scatter waves from Particle. One example medium is Acoustic. The shape completely defines the geometry and position of the particle, see Shapes for details.\n\nFor an example we can define a circular particle with acoustics medium:\n\njulia> using MultipleScattering;\n\njulia> mymedium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 10.0, c = 2.0); # 2D acoustics with density ρ = 10.0 and soundspeed c = 2.0\n\njulia> myshape = Sphere([-2.0, 2.0], 2.0);\n\njulia> p1 = Particle(mymedium, myshape)\nParticle(Acoustic(10.0, 2.0 + 0.0im, 2), Sphere{Float64, 2}([-2.0, 2.0], 2.0))\n\njulia> outer_radius(p1), origin(p1)\n(2.0, [-2.0, 2.0])\n\nIf only a radius is given, the particle is a circle (or sphere in 3D) centred at the origin:\n\njulia> Particle(mymedium, 0.5)\nParticle(Acoustic(10.0, 2.0 + 0.0im, 2), Sphere{Float64, 2}([0.0, 0.0], 0.5))\n\nThe medium of a particle can also describe a boundary condition. For example sound_hard (also called rigid) and sound_soft (also called pressure_release) define particles where the normal derivative of the pressure, or the pressure, is zero on the boundary.","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/particles/#Placing-particles-in-a-region","page":"Particles","text":"Suppose we want to place many circular particles in a region. The region has to be a pre-defined Shape, for example a Circle:\n\n# Define the region\ncentre = [0.0, 0.0];\nbig_radius = 3.0;\ncircle = Sphere(centre, big_radius);\n\n# Define the particle geometry and medium\np_radius = 0.3;\nmyshape = Circle(p_radius); # we do not specify centre as it is not used by random_particles\nmymedium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 10.0, c = 2.0);\n\n# Generate particles inside circle\nvolfrac = 0.2;\nparticles = random_particles(mymedium, myshape;\n    region_shape = circle,\n    volume_fraction = volfrac,\n    seed = 1\n);\n\nusing Plots;\n\nplot(particles)\nplot!(circle, linecolor = :red)\n\n(Image: Particles in circle)","title":"Placing particles in a region"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/particles/#Placing-polydisperse-particles-in-a-region","page":"Particles","text":"Similar to the above, we can place particles with a range of shapes.\n\nSuppose we want to add to the above example a range of smaller particles:\n\n\n# Define a range of particles sizes\nrs = [0.05, 0.15, 0.15, 0.2]; # by repeating the radius 0.15 twice, there will be twice as many particles with this radius.\nmyshapes = Circle.(rs);\nmymedium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 0.2, c = 0.2);\n\n# Generate particles inside circle\nvolfrac = 0.15\npolydisperse_particles = random_particles(mymedium, myshapes;\n    current_particles = particles,\n    region_shape = circle,\n    volume_fraction = volfrac,\n    seed = 1\n);\n\nplot(polydisperse_particles, linecolor = :green)\nplot!(particles, linewidth = 2.0)\nplot!(circle, linecolor = :red)\n\n(Image: Particles in circle)","title":"Placing polydisperse particles in a region"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/particles/#Removing-particles","page":"Particles","text":"Say we want to place a point-source within region filled with particles. To avoid placing the source inside any particle, we can remove a small region of particles:\n\nsmall_circle = Circle(1.2);\n\nfilter!(p -> !(p ⊆ small_circle), polydisperse_particles)\n\nplot(polydisperse_particles, linecolor = :green)\nplot!(circle, linecolor = :red)\n\n(Image: Particles in circle)\n\nNext we place a point source in the centre and plot the result, while excluding a small region Circle(0.1) to avoid the singularity caused by a point source:\n\nω = 0.4;\npoint_wave = point_source(Acoustic(2; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0), [0.0, 0.0]);\nsim = FrequencySimulation(polydisperse_particles, point_wave);\nplot(sim, ω;\n        resolution = 20,\n        exclude_region = Circle(0.1),\n        drawparticles = true\n)\n\n(Image: Particles in circle)","title":"Removing particles"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/particles/#Particle-internals","page":"Particles","text":"To define the scattering from a particle we use the T-matrix method, see What run calculates. This package provides the T-matrix for acoustic particles with the shapes:\n\ncircles and spheres, which are exact and include penetrable, sound-hard, and sound-soft particles;\na circle inside another circle, with the same origin, given by a CapsuleParticle;\n2D Helmholtz resonators, IsotropicHelmholtz and Helmholtz, which are sound-hard.\n\nTo understand how to define a new T-matrix read Notes on the T-matrix, see New particles, and see the source code of t_matrix.","title":"Particle internals"},{"category":"section","location":"example/random_particles/README/#Simple-random-particles-example","page":"Simple random particles example","text":"\n## Define particle properties\nDefine the volume fraction of particles, the region to place the particles, and their radius\n\njulia using MultipleScattering num_particles = 4 radius = 1.0\n\nparticlemedium = Acoustic(2; ρ=0.2, c=0.1) # 2D particle with density ρ = 0.2 and soundspeed c = 0.1 particleshape = Circle(radius)\n\nmaxwidth = 20*radius bottomleft = [0.,-maxwidth] topright = [maxwidth,maxwidth] region_shape = Box([bottomleft,topright])\n\nparticles = randomparticles(particlemedium, particleshape; regionshape = regionshape, numparticles = num_particles)\n\n\nNow choose the receiver position `x`, the host medium, set plane wave as a source wave, and choose the angular frequency range `ωs`\n\njulia x = [-10.,0.] hostmedium = Acoustic(2; ρ=1.0, c=1.0) source =  planesource(host_medium; position = x, direction = [1.0,0.])\n\nωs = LinRange(0.01,1.0,100)\n\nsimulation = FrequencySimulation(particles, source) result = run(simulation, x, ωs)\n\n\nWe use the `Plots` package to plot both the response at the listener position x\n\n\njulia     using Plots; #pyplot(linewidth = 2.0)     plot(result, fieldapply=real) # plot result     plot!(result, fieldapply=imag)     #savefig(\"plot_result.png\")\n\n![Plot of response against wavenumber](plot_result.png)\n\nAnd plot the whole field inside the region_shape `bounds` for a specific wavenumber (`ω=0.8`)\n\njulia     bottomleft = [-15.,-maxwidth]     topright = [maxwidth,max_width]     bounds = Box([bottomleft,topright])\n\n#plot(simulation,0.8; res=80, bounds=bounds)\n#plot!(region_shape, linecolor=:red)\n#plot!(simulation)\n#scatter!([x[1]],[x[2]], lab=\"receiver\")\n\n#savefig(\"plot_field.png\")\n\n``` (Image: Plot real part of acoustic field)","title":"Simple random particles example"},{"category":"section","location":"example/random_particles/README/#Things-to-try","page":"Simple random particles example","text":"Try changing the volume fraction, particle radius and ω values we evaluate","title":"Things to try"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#Base","page":"Base","text":"Pages = [\"base.md\"]","title":"Base"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#base_physical_property","page":"Base","text":"The types and functions shared between all different types of physical mediums.","title":"Physical mediums"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#base_particle","page":"Base","text":"Particle types and functions.","title":"Particle"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#base_symmetries","page":"Base","text":"Symmetries shared between the source and the material or particles. These can be used to simplify calculations.","title":"Symmetries"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#source_base","page":"Base","text":"RegularSource types and functions for all physical mediums.","title":"RegularSource"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#impulse_base","page":"Base","text":"For convert to the time domain.","title":"Impulse"},{"category":"section","location":"library/base/#simulation_base","page":"Base","text":"Simulation types and functions.","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.PhysicalMedium","page":"Base","text":"PhysicalMedium{Dim,FieldDim}\n\nAn abstract type used to represent the physical medium, the dimension of the field, and the number of spatial dimensions. We expect every sub-type of PhysicalMedium{Dim,1} to have a field c which is the complex wave speed. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.PhysicalMedium"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.ScalarMedium","page":"Base","text":"ScalarMedium{Dim}\n\nAn type used to represent a scalar wave which satisfies a Helmoholtz equations. Dim represents the number of spatial dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.ScalarMedium"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.basislength_to_basisorder-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{Type{P}, Int64}} where P<:PhysicalMedium{3, 1}","page":"Base","text":"basislength_to_basisorder(P::Type{<:PhysicalMedium}, len::Int)\n\nThe inverse of basisorder_to_basislength.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.basislength_to_basisorder"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.basisorder_to_basislength-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{Type{P}, Int64}} where P<:PhysicalMedium{3, 1}","page":"Base","text":"basisorder_to_basislength(P::Type{<:PhysicalMedium}, order::Int)\n\nThe number of basis functions, of the type of medium P, with basis order up to order. For scalar waves this is 2*order + 1 in 2D and (order + 1)^2 in 3D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.basisorder_to_basislength"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.boundary_data","page":"Base","text":"A tuples of vectors of the field close to the boundary of the shape. The field is calculated from sim::FrequencySimulation, but the PhysicalMedium inside and outside of the shape are assumed to be given by insidemedium and outsidemedium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.boundary_data"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.field_dimension-Union{Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim, FieldDim}}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim}","page":"Base","text":"Extract the dimension of the field of this physical property\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.field_dimension"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.field_dimension-Union{Tuple{Type{P}}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim, P<:PhysicalMedium{Dim, FieldDim}}","page":"Base","text":"Extract the dimension of the field of this type of physical property\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.field_dimension"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.internal_field","page":"Base","text":"the field inside an AbstractParticle a some given point x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.internal_field"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.outgoing_basis_function","page":"Base","text":"Basis of outgoing wave. A series expansion in this basis should converge to any scattered field outside of a ball which contains the scatterer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.outgoing_basis_function"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.outgoing_radial_basis","page":"Base","text":"Basis of outgoing wave when the dependance on the angles has been removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.outgoing_radial_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.outgoing_translation_matrix-Union{Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim, 1}, Integer, Integer, Number, AbstractVector}} where Dim","page":"Base","text":"outgoing_translation_matrix(medium::PhysicalMedium, in_order, out_order, ω, d)\n\nReturns the matrix U which translates the outgoing basis into the regular basis. That is, U * vs(in_order, x) ≈ us(out_order, x + d) for |x| < |d|, where vs = regular_basis_function(medium, ω) and us = outgoing_basis_function(medium, ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.outgoing_translation_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.regular_basis_function","page":"Base","text":"A basis for regular functions, that is, smooth functions. A series expansion in this basis should converge to any regular function within a ball.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.regular_basis_function"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.regular_translation_matrix-Union{Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim, 1}, Integer, Integer, Number, AbstractVector}} where Dim","page":"Base","text":"regular_translation_matrix(medium::PhysicalMedium, in_order, out_order, ω, d)\n\nReturns the matrix V which translates the regular basis. That is, V * vs(in_order, x) ≈ vs(out_order, x + d), where vs = regular_basis_function(medium, ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.regular_translation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.spatial_dimension-Union{Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim, FieldDim}}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim}","page":"Base","text":"Extract the dimension of the space that this physical property lives in\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.spatial_dimension"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.spatial_dimension-Union{Tuple{Type{P}}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim, P<:PhysicalMedium{Dim, FieldDim}}","page":"Base","text":"Extract the dimension of the space that this type of physical property lives in\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.spatial_dimension"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.complex_legendre_array-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, Union{Complex{T}, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"complex_legendre_array(l_max::Int, z::Complex{T})\n\nreturns a vector of all associated legendre functions with degree l <= l_max. The degree and order (indices) of the elements of the vector are given by spherical_harmonics_indices(l_max::Int).\n\nThe associated legendre polynomials are taken from the package AssociatedLegendrePolynomials.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.complex_legendre_array"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffbessel-Tuple{Function, Number, Any, Int64}","page":"Base","text":"diffbessel(f::Function,m,x,n::Int)\n\nDifferentiates 'n' times any bessel function 'f' of order 'm' and at the argument 'x'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffbessel"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffbesselj-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Number, T, Int64}} where T<:Number","page":"Base","text":"m-th Derivative of Bessel function of the first kind\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffbesselj"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffbesselj-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Number, T}} where T<:Number","page":"Base","text":"Derivative of Bessel function of first kind\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffbesselj"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffhankelh1-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Base","text":"Derivative of Hankel function of the first kind\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffhankelh1"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffhankelh1-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Number, T, Int64}} where T<:Number","page":"Base","text":"diffhankelh1(m,x,n::Int)\n\nDifferentiates 'n' times the hankelh1 function of order 'm' and at the argument 'x'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffhankelh1"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.diffsbessel-Tuple{Function, Number, Number}","page":"Base","text":"diffsbessel(f::Function,m,x)\n\nDifferentiates the spherical bessel function 'f' (for any spherical bessel). The order is 'm' and the argument is 'x'. Note 'x' can be a complex number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.diffsbessel"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.gaunt_coefficient-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractFloat}, Vararg{Int64, 6}}","page":"Base","text":"gaunt_coefficient(l1,m1,l2,m2,l3,m3)\n\nA version of the Gaunt coefficients which are used to write the product of two spherical harmonics. If Y_{l,m} is a complex spherical harmonic, with the typical phase conventions from quantum mechanics, then:\n\ngaunt_coefficient(l1,m1,l2,m2,l3,m3) = 4*π*im^{l2+l3-l1} Integral[Y_{l1,m1}*conj(Y_{l2,m2})*conj(Y_{l3,m3})]\n\nwhere the integral is over the solid angle. In the papers by Art Gower, this symbol is denoted by c_{(l1,m1),(l2,m2)(l3,m3)}.\n\nThe most standard gaunt coefficients G(l1,m1;l2,m2;l3) are related through the identity:\n\n4pi * G(l1,m1;l2,m2;l3) = im^(l1-l2-l3) * (-1)^m2 * gaunt_coefficient(l1,m1,l2,-m2,l3,m1+m2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.gaunt_coefficient"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.gaunt_cross_coefficient-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractFloat}, Vararg{Int64, 6}}","page":"Base","text":"gaunt_cross_coefficient(l1,m1,l2,m2,l3,m3)\n\nSimilar to the Gaunt coefficient, but use for vector spherical harmonics, and specifically how one shear wave coverts to the other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.gaunt_cross_coefficient"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.sbesselj-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Number, T}} where T","page":"Base","text":"sbesselj(m,x)\n\nReturns the spherical besselj function. The order is 'm' and the argument is 'x'. Note 'x' can be a complex number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.sbesselj"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.shankelh1-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Number, T}} where T","page":"Base","text":"shankelh1(m,x)\n\nReturns the spherical hankel function of the first kind. The order is 'm' and the argument is 'x'. Note 'x' can be a complex number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.shankelh1"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, Complex{T}, Union{Complex{T}, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"spherical_harmonics(l_max::Int, θ::Complex{T}, φ::Union{T,Complex{T}})\n\nreturns a vector of all spherical harmonics with degree l <= l_max with complex arguments. The degree and order (indices) of the elements of the vector are given by spherical_harmonics_indices(l_max::Int).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, T, Union{Complex{T}, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"spherical_harmonics(l_max::Int, θ::T, φ::T)\n\nReturns a vector of all the spherial harmonics Y_(lm)(thetaphi) for all the degrees l and orders m. The degree and order (indices) of the elements of the vector are given by spherical_harmonics_indices(l_max::Int).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics_dθ-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"spherical_harmonics_dθ(l_max::Int, θ::T, φ::T)\n\nReturns a vector of all partial Y_(lm)(thetaphi)  partial theta for all the degrees l and orders m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.spherical_harmonics_dθ"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.Electromagnetic","page":"Base","text":"Electromagnetic\n\nRepresents the physical properties for a homogenous isotropic electromagnetic medium. Produces a three dimensional vector field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Electromagnetic"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractParticle","page":"Base","text":"Object we can scatter waves off\n\nSubtypes will contain information about shape and material properties. Most crucially, they will implement the t_matrix function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractParticle"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.CapsuleParticle","page":"Base","text":"CapsuleParticle(outer::Particle, inner::Particle)\n\nA particle within another particle, both with the same shape type and origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.CapsuleParticle"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.Particle","page":"Base","text":"Particle(medium::PhysicalMedium, shape::Shape)\n\nCreate particle with inner medium and shape (dimensions must agree).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Particle"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.Particle-Union{Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim}, Any}} where Dim","page":"Base","text":"Particle(medium, radius)\n\nReturns a particle shaped like a sphere or circle, when the particle shape is not given and with the specified radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Particle"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.in-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractParticle}","page":"Base","text":"in(vector, particle)::Bool\n\nReturns true if vector is in interior of shape of particle, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.issubset-Tuple{AbstractParticle, AbstractParticle}","page":"Base","text":"issubset(shape1, shape2)::Bool\n\nReturns true if shape1 is entirely contained within shape2, false otherwise (also works with particles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.issubset-Tuple{AbstractParticle, Shape}","page":"Base","text":"issubset(shape1, shape2)::Bool\n\nReturns true if shape1 is entirely contained within shape2, false otherwise (also works with particles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.issubset-Tuple{Shape, AbstractParticle}","page":"Base","text":"issubset(shape1, shape2)::Bool\n\nReturns true if shape1 is entirely contained within shape2, false otherwise (also works with particles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.:≅-Tuple{AbstractParticle, AbstractParticle}","page":"Base","text":"iscongruent(p1::AbstractParticle, p2::AbstractParticle)::Bool\n≅(p1::AbstractParticle, p2::AbstractParticle)::Bool\n\nReturns true if medium and shape of particles are the same, ignoring origin, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.:≅"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.estimate_outgoing_basisorder-Tuple{PhysicalMedium, Particle, Number}","page":"Base","text":"estimate_outgoing_basisorder(medium::PhysicalMedium, p::Particle, ω::Number; tol = 1e-6)\n\nEstimate the basis order needed to represent the field scattered by the particle p. This is the smallest basis order such that increasing it changes the (normalised) diagonal of the T-matrix by less than tol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.estimate_outgoing_basisorder"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.iscongruent-Tuple{AbstractParticle, AbstractParticle}","page":"Base","text":"iscongruent(p1::AbstractParticle, p2::AbstractParticle)::Bool\n≅(p1::AbstractParticle, p2::AbstractParticle)::Bool\n\nReturns true if medium and shape of particles are the same, ignoring origin, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.iscongruent"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.Symmetry","page":"Base","text":"Symmetry(T)\nSymmetry(T1,T2)\n\nreturns the symmetry of the object T, or the combined symmetry of the two objects T1 and T2. Some possible symmetries include PlanarSymmetry,  RadialSymmetry,  and WithoutSymmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Symmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"AbstractSymmetry\n\nAn abstract types which dictates the symmetry of the setup. That is, the symmetry shared between the incident wave and the shape of the material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.WithoutSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"WithoutSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe materials and incident waves which share no common symmetry. This will lead to the most general regular wave expansion for the eignvectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.WithoutSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractPlanarSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"An incident plane-wave and halfspace material will result in all fields being plane-waves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractPlanarSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.PlanarSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"PlanarSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe materials and incident waves that both share a planar symmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.PlanarSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.PlanarAzimuthalSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"PlanarAzimuthalSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe a materail and incident wave which have both PlanarSymmetry and AzimuthalSymmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.PlanarAzimuthalSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractAzimuthalSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"For spatial dimension > 2, we can consider problems that have azimuthal symmetry. For example, a plane-wave incident on a sphere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractAzimuthalSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AzimuthalSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"AzimuthalSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe materials and incident waves in 3 spatial dimensions that share a symmetry about the azimuth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AzimuthalSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.RadialSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"RadialSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe materials and incident waves in that are both radially symmetric. That is, the material is a sphere, and the incident wave is radially symmetric around the center of this spherical material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.RadialSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractTranslationSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"An incident plane-wave and infinite cylinder material will result in all fields being cylindrical Bessel waves with a phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractTranslationSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.TranslationSymmetry","page":"Base","text":"TranslationSymmetry\n\nA type used to describe materials and incident waves that both share a translation symmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.TranslationSymmetry"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.AbstractSource","page":"Base","text":"Represent any source (incident) wave\n\nSubtypes may have a symmetry (such as PlaneSource) and will contain information about physical medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractSource"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.PlaneSource","page":"Base","text":"PlaneSource(medium::P, amplitude::SVector, direction::SVector)\n\nIs a struct type which describes a plane-wave source that drives/forces the whole system. It has three fields: a physical medium, an amplitude of the source, and the direction the propagate in direction.\n\nFor any given angular frequency ω, the PlaneSource has the value e^i ωc mathbf v cdot mathbf x  at the point mathbf x, where c is the medium wavespeed and mathbf v is the direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.PlaneSource"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.RegularSource","page":"Base","text":"RegularSource(medium::P, field::Function, coef::Function)\n\nIs a struct type which describes the source field that drives/forces the whole system. It is also known as an incident wave. It has three fields RegularSource.medium, RegularSource.field, and RegularSource.coefficients.\n\nThe source field at the position 'x' and angular frequency 'ω' is given by\n\nx = [1.0,0.0]\nω = 1.0\nRegularSource.field(x,ω)\n\nThe field RegularSource.coefficients(order,centre,ω) returns the coefficients of the source field when expanded in the regular basis regular_basis_function centred at centre, up to the basis order order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.RegularSource"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.regular_spherical_coefficients-Tuple{RegularSource}","page":"Base","text":"regular_spherical_coefficients(source::RegularSource)\n\nreturn a function which can calculate the coefficients of a regular spherical wave basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.regular_spherical_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.regular_spherical_source-Union{Tuple{CT}, Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{PhysicalMedium{Dim}, AbstractVector{CT}}} where {T, Dim, CT<:Union{Complex{T}, T}}","page":"Base","text":"regular_spherical_source(PhysicalMedium,regular_coefficients; amplitude = one(T), position = zeros(T,Dim))\n\nc_n =regular_coefficients[n], x_o=position, and let v_n(kx) represent the regular spherical basis with wavenumber k at position x. The above returns a source where source.field(x,ω) =sum_n c_n v_n(kx -k x_0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.regular_spherical_source"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.self_test-Union{Tuple{RegularSource{P}}, Tuple{P}} where P","page":"Base","text":"Check that the source functions return the correct types\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.self_test"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.source_expand-Tuple{AbstractSource, AbstractVector}","page":"Base","text":"source_expand(RegularSource, centre; basis_order = 4)\n\nReturns a function of (x,ω) which approximates the value of the source at (x,ω). That is, the source is written in terms of a regular basis expansion centred at centre.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.source_expand"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.ContinuousImpulse","page":"Base","text":"See also: DiscreteImpulse, frequency_to_time\n\nContinuousImpulse{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nA struct used to represent an analytic impulse function. Has two fields: in_time a function of time t, and in_freq a function of the angular frequency ω. in_freq should be the Fourier transform of in_time, though this is not enforced.\n\nWe use the Fourier transform convention: F(ω) =  ∫ f(t)exp(imωt) dt, f(t) = (2π)^(-1) * ∫ F(ω)exp(-imωt) dω.\n\nAn impluse f(t) is convoluted in time with the field u(t), however we avoid the convolution by working with the fourier transform F(ω) of the impulse f(t), which results in\n\nfrequency to time: (2π)^(-1) * ∫ F(ω)U(ω)exp(-imωt) dω\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.ContinuousImpulse"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.DiscreteImpulse","page":"Base","text":"See also: ContinuousImpulse, frequency_to_time, DiscreteGaussianImpulse\n\nDiscreteImpulse{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nA struct used to represent a numerical impulse. Only the fields: in_freq which is the frequency response vector, and the frequency vector ω are required to use this struct to use in the function frequency_to_time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.DiscreteImpulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.DiscreteGaussianImpulse-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Base","text":"See also: ContinuousImpulse, TimeDiracImpulse\n\nDiscreteGaussianImpulse(t_vec[, ω_vec])\n\nReturns a discretised gaussian impulse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.DiscreteGaussianImpulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.FreqDiracImpulse-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"Dirac Delta function of unit area in the frequency domain centred at ω=ω0.\n\nWarning: in frequency space this is a singuarity and so may lead to unexpected behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.FreqDiracImpulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.GaussianImpulse-Tuple{T} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"See also: ContinuousImpulse, TimeDiracImpulse\n\nGaussianImpulse(maxω[; σ = 3.0/maxω^2])\n\nReturns a gaussian impulse function, which in the frequency domain is exp(-σ*ω^2)*(2sqrt(σ*pi)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.GaussianImpulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.TimeDiracImpulse-Tuple{T} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"TimeDiracImpulse(t0::T)\n\nDirac Delta function of unit area in the time domain centred at t=t0.\n\nWarning: in the time domain this is a singuarity and so may lead to unexpected behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.TimeDiracImpulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.continuous_to_discrete_impulse-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{ContinuousImpulse{T}, AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{ContinuousImpulse{T}, AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Base","text":"continuous_to_discrete_impulse(impulse::ContinuousImpulse, t_vec, ω_vec = t_to_ω(t_vec); t_shift = 0.0, ω_shift = 0.0)\n\nReturns a DiscreteImpulse by sampling impulse. The signal can be shifted in time and frequency by choosing t_shift and ω_shift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.continuous_to_discrete_impulse"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.self_test-Union{Tuple{ContinuousImpulse{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Base","text":"Check that the continuous impulse functions return the correct types\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.self_test"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.self_test-Union{Tuple{DiscreteImpulse{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Base","text":"Check that the discrete impulse vectors are the right sizes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.self_test"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.firstnonzero-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"Returns the first element of array which isn't zero (assumes elements are increasing and distinct)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.firstnonzero"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.frequency_to_time-Union{Tuple{FrequencySimulationResult{T, Dim, FieldDim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim, T}","page":"Base","text":"Convert a FrequencySimulationResult into a TimeSimulationResult by using the inverse fourier transform. Assumes only positive frequencies and a real time signal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.frequency_to_time"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.frequency_to_time-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{Complex{T}}, AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{AbstractArray{Complex{T}}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"frequency_to_time(field_mat::AbstractArray, ω_vec::AbstractVector,\n    t_vec::AbstractArray = ω_to_t(ω_vec);\n    method = :DFT,\n    impulse::ContinuousImpulse = TimeDiracImpulse(zero(T)),\n    discrete_impulse::DiscreteImpulse = continuous_to_discrete_impulse(impulse, t_vec, ω_vec))\n\nSee also: DiscreteImpulse, ContinuousImpulse\n\nCalculates the time response from the frequency response by approximating an inverse Fourier transform. The time signal is assumed to be real and the frequenices ω_vec are assumed to be positive (can include zero) and sorted. The result is convoluted in time with the user specified impulse.\n\nWe use the Fourier transform convention: F(ω) =  ∫ f(t)exp(imωt) dt f(t) = (2π)^(-1) * ∫ F(ω)exp(-imωt) dt\n\nTo easily sample any time, the default is not FFT, but a discrete version of the transform above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.frequency_to_time"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.t_to_ω-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"The inverse of ωtot if ω_vec[1] == 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_to_ω"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.time_to_frequency-Union{Tuple{TimeSimulationResult{T, Dim, FieldDim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{FieldDim}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, FieldDim, T}","page":"Base","text":"Convert a TimeSimulationResult into a FrequencySimulationResult by using the fourier transform. Assumes only positive frequencies and a real time signal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.time_to_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.time_to_frequency-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}}}, AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{Union{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}}}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"function time_to_frequency(field_mat::AbstractArray, t_vec::AbstractVector,\n    ω_vec::AbstractArray = t_to_ω(t_vec);\n    method = :DFT,\n    impulse::ContinuousImpulse = TimeDiracImpulse(zero(T)),\n    discrete_impulse::DiscreteImpulse = continuous_to_discrete_impulse(impulse, t_vec, ω_vec)\n)\n\nThe inverse of the function frequencytotime (only an exact inverse when using :DFT integration). We use the Fourier transform convention: F(ω) =  ∫ f(t)exp(imω*t) dt\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.time_to_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.ω_to_t-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Base","text":"returns an array of time from the frequency array ωvec. Uses the same convention for sampling the time as the discrete Fourier transfrom. Assumes ωvec is ordered and non-negative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.ω_to_t"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.FrequencySimulation","page":"Base","text":"FrequencySimulation([particles::AbstractParticles=[],]\n                    source::AbstractSource)\n\nBuild a FrequencySimulation. If particles are not provided, an empty array is used.\n\nAfter building, you can run the simulation to get a FrequencySimulationResult.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.FrequencySimulation"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.run-Tuple{FrequencySimulation}","page":"Base","text":"run(sim::FrequencySimulation, x, ω; basis_order=5)\n\nRun the simulation sim for the position x and angular frequency ω.\n\nPosition can be an SVector or Vector{SVector} and frequency can be a float or vector of floats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.run"},{"category":"method","location":"library/base/#Base.run-Tuple{FrequencySimulation, Shape, AbstractVector}","page":"Base","text":"run(sim::FrequencySimulation, region::Shape;\n    res=20, xres=res, yres=res, basis_order=5)\n\nRun the simulation sim for a grid of positions in region and for angular frequency ω.\n\nFrequency can be a float or vector of floats. The resolution of the grid points is defined by xres and yres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.run"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.overlapping_pairs","page":"Base","text":"overlapping_pairs(particles::AbstractParticles)\noverlapping_pairs(origins, radii)\n\nReturns the pairs of indices [i, j] of the particles which overlap, where two particles overlap if the distance between their origins is less than the sum of their outer radiuses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.overlapping_pairs"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.SimulationResult","page":"Base","text":"Abstract class for Results of Simulations\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.SimulationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.FrequencySimulationResult","page":"Base","text":"Struct to hold results of a FrequencySimulation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.FrequencySimulationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.TimeSimulationResult","page":"Base","text":"Struct to hold results of a simulation through time\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.TimeSimulationResult"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.basis_coefficients","page":"Base","text":"basis_coefficients(sim::FrequencySimulation, ω::AbstractFloat; basis_order::Int=5)::Matrix{Complex}\n\nReturn coefficients for bases around each particle for a given simulation and angular frequency (ω).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.basis_coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.field","page":"Base","text":"field(result::SimulationResult, [i::Integer, j::Integer])\n\nGet field from result, optionally specifying indices.\n\nReturns single value of/matrix of complex SVectors() if vector field, and complex float if scalar field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.field"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.scattering_matrix","page":"Base","text":"Create the matrix S which will be inverted to find the scattering coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.scattering_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix","page":"Base","text":"t_matrix(particle, medium, ω, order)\n\nReturns a finite T-matrix, with size depending on order, for a specific particle within a medium with specific physical properties.\n\n\n\n\n\nt_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Sphere{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D circlular acoustic particle in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\nt_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},IsotropicHelmholtz{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D isotropic Helmholtz resonator in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\nt_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Helmholtz{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D non-isotropic Helmholtz resonator in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\nt_matrix(CapsuleParticle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Sphere{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D circlular capsule particle in an acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\nt_matrix(Particle{3,Acoustic{T,3},Sphere{T,3}}, Acoustic{T,3}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 3D spherical acoustic particle in a 3D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"library/base/#MultipleScattering.get_t_matrices","page":"Base","text":"get_t_matrices(PhysicalMedium, Vector{Particles}, ω, basis_order::Integer)\n\nReturns vector of T-matrices from a vector of particles in a specific domain. Can save computation if multiple of the same kind of particle are present in the vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.get_t_matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"example/moments/README/#StatisticalMoments","page":"StatisticalMoments","text":"Here we are going to simulate the scattered wave for many different configurations of particles. We can then take the average and standard deviation (the moments) of the scattered wave. In statistical mechanics this process is called ensemble average.","title":"StatisticalMoments"},{"category":"section","location":"example/moments/README/#Region-and-particles-properties","page":"StatisticalMoments","text":"First we choose the region to place particles and the receiver position:\n\nusing MultipleScattering\nbottomleft = [0.0;-25.0]\ntopright = [50.0;25.0]\nshape = Box([bottomleft, topright])\nx = [-10.0,0.0]\n\nusing Plots\nplot(shape);\nscatter!([x[1]],[x[2]], label=\"\");\nplot_shape = annotate!([(x[1], x[2] -2., \"Receiver\")])\n\n(Image: Plot of shape and receiver)\n\nNext we fill this shape with a random (uniform distribution) configuration of particles:\n\nvolfrac = 0.05\nradius = 1.0\n\nparticles = random_particles(Acoustic(2; ρ=0.0, c=0.0), Circle(radius);\n        region_shape = shape,\n        volume_fraction = volfrac,\n        seed=2\n);\n\nlength(particles)\n\n# output\n40\n\nTo see the position of the chosen particles:\n\nplot(plot_shape)\nplot!(particles);\nplot!()\n\n(Image: Plot particles)\n\nScattering a plane-wave from these particles  \n\nωs = LinRange(0.01,1.0,100)\nplane_wave = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2);\n    direction = [1.0,0.0], position = x);\n\nplot(run(particles, plane_wave,x,ωs))\n\n(Image: )","title":"Region and particles properties"},{"category":"section","location":"example/moments/README/#The-moments-of-the-scattered-wave","page":"StatisticalMoments","text":"Now we will do simulations for particles placed in many different configurations and take the moments:\n\nresults = map(1:20) do i\n    particles = random_particles(Acoustic(2; ρ=0.0, c=0.0), Circle(radius);\n            region_shape = shape,\n            volume_fraction = volfrac,\n            seed=i\n    )\n    run(FrequencySimulation(particles, plane_wave), x, ωs)\nend\n\n# package Plots changed it's argument, the below no longer works..\n# num_moments = 3\n# plot(results; field_apply = real, num_moments = num_moments)\n# plot!(xlabel=\"wavenumbers\", title=\"Moments of the real part\")\n\n(Image: Moments of the real part the scattered waves)","title":"The moments of the scattered wave"},{"category":"section","location":"example/moments/README/#Calculate-the-moments-of-the-scattered-wave-in-time","page":"StatisticalMoments","text":"time_simulations = frequency_to_time.(results)\ntime_simulations[1].t # the time_arr chosen will be based on the discrete Fourier transform of simulations[1].k_arr\n# real_time_moments = StatisticalMoments(time_simulations; response_apply=real) # moments of the real part\n# plot(real_time_moments,xlims=(0,300));\n# plot!(xlabel=\"time\", title=\"Moments of the real part of the time wave\")\n\n(Image: Moments of the real part the scattered waves in time)","title":"Calculate the moments of the scattered wave in time"},{"category":"section","location":"example/moments/README/#References","page":"StatisticalMoments","text":"A. L. Gower, R. M. Gower, J. Deakin, W. J. Parnell, I. D. Abrahams, Learning about random media from near-surface backscattering: using machine learning to measure particle size and concentration, arXiv preprint, (2018)1801.05490","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/plot/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"The plotting for this package is supplied by the package Plots. The options and keywords used for the package Plots can be used for the plots in this package.\n\nBelow are examples of plotting the whole field in frequency (harmonic wave) and time. The examples require the package Plots.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/plot/#Field-Harmonic-two-gaps","page":"Plotting","text":"using MultipleScattering\nusing Plots; gr(size = (800,300))\n\nradius = 1\nω = 2.0\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2)\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(0.0, 0.0, 2)\n\n# Create a wall of particles\nparticles = [\n  Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([0.,y],1.0))\nfor y = -40:2*radius:40.]\n\n# Make two gaps in the wall\ndeleteat!(particles,[18,19,23,24])\n\n# Define region to plot\nbottomleft = [-10.;-15.]\ntopright = [30.;15.]\nregion = Box([bottomleft, topright])\n\n# Calculating scattering for a plane wave\nsource =  plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0,0.0])\n\n# You can skip the step of defining FrequencySimulation\nresult = run(particles, source, region, [ω]; res=100)\n\nplot(result,ω;\n    field_apply = abs, seriestype = :heatmap,\n    title = \"Absolute value\"\n  );\np1 = plot!(particles, ylims = (-15.0,15.0));  \nplot(result,ω;\n    field_apply = real, seriestype = :heatmap,\n    title = \"Real part\"\n);\np2 = plot!(particles, ylims = (-15.0,15.0));\nplot(p1, p2)\n\n# savefig(\"gap-diffraction.png\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Field - Harmonic two gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/plot/#Movie-Harmonic-two-gaps","page":"Plotting","text":"Continuing from Field - Harmonic two gaps, the previous example, we can plot how the harmonic field oscillates in time. That is, to get the harmonic field at time t we just multiple the field by mathrm e^-mathrm i omega t for every mathbf x. For example, the plane wave mathrm e^mathrm i x k would become mathrm e^mathrm i x k -mathrm i omega t.   \n\ngr(size = (450,300))\n\nts = LinRange(0.,2pi/ω,30)\n\nmaxc = round(10*maximum(real.(field(result))))/10\nminc = round(10*minimum(real.(field(result))))/10\n\nanim = @animate for t in ts\n    plot(result,ω; seriestype = :heatmap,\n        phase_time=t, clim=(minc,maxc),\n        ylims = (-15.0,15.0) , c=:balance\n    )\n    plot!(particles)\n    plot!(colorbar=false, title=\"\",axis=false, xguide =\"\",yguide =\"\")\nend\n# gif(anim,\"gap-diffraction.gif\", fps = 7)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Movie - Harmonic two gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/plot/#Movie-Time-impulse-plane-wave-two-gaps","page":"Plotting","text":"Continuing from Field - Harmonic two gaps, we can plot how an impulse plave-wave in time passes through two gaps. See Time response for more details on the code used below.\n\ngr(size = (450,300))\nωs = LinRange(0.0,2.0,300)[2:end] # avoid using ω = 0\n\n# We use a lower resolution (resolution = 50) as this is a heavier calculation\nresult = run(particles, source, region, ωs; res = 50)\n\n# Calculate time response over rect\nt_max = 0.75 .* real(region.dimensions[1] / host_medium.c)\nts = LinRange(0.0,t_max,75)\nimpulse = GaussianImpulse(maximum(ωs)*0.6)\ntimres = frequency_to_time(result; t_vec = ts, impulse = impulse)\n\nmaxc = round(10*maximum(field(timres)))/10\nminc = round(10*minimum(field(timres)))/10\n\n# timres = TimeSimulationResult(timres.field .+ max_c/100.0 , timres.x, timres.t)\n\nylimits =  (-region.dimensions[2]/2,region.dimensions[2]/2)\nanim = @animate for t in ts\n    plot(timres,t, seriestype=:heatmap,\n      clim = (minc, maxc),\n      leg = false, ylims = ylimits\n    )\n    plot!(particles)\n    plot!(frame = :none, title=\"\", xguide =\"\",yguide =\"\")\nend\n\n# gif(anim,\"gap-diffraction.gif\", fps = 7)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Movie - Time impulse plane-wave - two gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/plot/#Movie-Harmonic-from-random-particles","page":"Plotting","text":"using MultipleScattering\nusing Plots;\n\nnum_particles = 70\nradius = 1.0\nω = 1.0\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2)\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(0.2, 0.3, 2)\nparticle_shape = Circle(radius)\n\nmax_width = 50*radius\nbottomleft = [0.,-max_width]\ntopright = [max_width,max_width]\nshape = Box([bottomleft,topright])\n\nparticles = random_particles(particle_medium, particle_shape; region_shape = shape, num_particles = num_particles)\n\nsource =  plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0,0.5])\n\nsimulation = FrequencySimulation(particles, source)\n\nbottomleft = [-25.,-max_width]\nbounds = Box([bottomleft,topright])\nresult = run(simulation, bounds, [ω]; res=100)\n\nts = LinRange(0.,2pi/ω,30)\n\nmaxc = round(10*maximum(real.(field(result))))/10\nminc = round(10*minimum(real.(field(result))))/10\n\nanim = @animate for t in ts\n    plot(result,ω; seriestype = :heatmap, phase_time=t, clim=(minc,maxc), c=:balance)\n    plot!(simulation)\n    plot!(colorbar=false, title=\"\",axis=false, xguide =\"\",yguide =\"\")\nend\n# gif(anim,\"backscatter_harmonic.gif\", fps = 7)\n\n(Image: backscattering from harmonic wave)","title":"Movie - Harmonic from random particles"},{"category":"section","location":"example/time_response_single_particle/README/#Time-response-from-a-single-particle","page":"Time response from a single particle","text":"The code time_response_single_particle.jl calculates the response, in frequency and in time, from one circular particle which is hit by a plane wave pulse.\n\nThe plane wave starts at the origin and travels in the direction of the x-axis. The particle has radius 1 and is centred at x = 100. The listener is at the origin:\n\nusing MultipleScattering\n\n# Define the particle\nradius = 1.0\nparticle_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 10.0, c = 0.5)\nparticles = [Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([100.0, 0.0], radius))]\n\n# Define the source wave\nhost_medium = Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2)\nsource = plane_source(host_medium; position = [0.0, 0.0], direction = [1.0, 0.0])\n\nNext we calculate the response at the listener for many frequencies. Larger frequencies need a larger basis order, so the basis order increases from min_basis_order = 1, for the smallest frequency, up to basis_order = 14, for the largest frequency, see Simulations and results:\n\nωs = LinRange(0.0, 50.0, 2000)\nfreq_result = run(particles, source, [0.0, 0.0], ωs; min_basis_order = 1, basis_order = 14)\n\nTo calculate the response in time, we use a Gaussian impulse which is very narrow in time, see Time response:\n\ntime_result = frequency_to_time(freq_result; impulse = GaussianImpulse(1.0; σ = 10/maximum(ωs)^2))\n\nFinally, we plot the results:\n\nusing Plots\n\np1 = plot(freq_result, title = \"Frequency response\")\np2 = plot(time_result, xlims = (-5, 230), title = \"Time response\")\np3 = plot(time_result, xlims = (190, 215), ylims = (-0.03, 0.07), title = \"Reflected pulses\")\nplot(p1, p2, p3, layout = (1, 3), size = (1000, 300), legend = false)\n\n(Image: Frequency and time response)\n\nIn frequency, the response oscillates around 1, which is the value of the incident plane wave at the origin. In time, the incident pulse is at the listener at time t = 0. The pulse then takes the time t = 99 to reach the particle, as the sound speed of the host medium is 1, and the same time to come back. This is why the first reflected pulse arrives at t = 198. Part of the pulse also travels through the particle, which has sound speed 0.5, and is reflected from the back of the particle. This takes the additional time 2 times 2  05 = 8, which leads to the second reflected pulse at t = 206.\n\nnote: Note\nThe time response is periodic with the period 2pi  Delta omega approx 251, where Delta omega is the step between the frequencies ωs. This is why we only plot up to t = 230, see the tip at the end of Time response.","title":"Time response from a single particle"},{"category":"section","location":"library/acoustics/#Acoustic","page":"Acoustic","text":"Acoustic type and functions.","title":"Acoustic"},{"category":"type","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.Acoustic","page":"Acoustic","text":"Acoustic{T<:AbstractFloat,Dim}(ρ::T, c::Complex{T})\nAcoustic(ρ::T, c::Union{T,Complex{AbstractFloat}}, Dim::Integer)\n\nPhysical properties for a homogenous isotropic acoustic medium with wavespeed (c) and density (ρ)\n\nSimulations in this medium produce scalar (1D) fields in Dim dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Acoustic"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.check_material-Tuple{Particle, Acoustic}","page":"Acoustic","text":"check_material(p::Particle, outer_medium::Acoustic)\n\nChecks if wave scattering from the particle p is physically viable given the material properties of p and its surrounding medium outer_medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.check_material"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.hard-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"hard(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_hard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.hard"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.impedance-Tuple{Acoustic}","page":"Acoustic","text":"impedance(medium::Acoustic)\n\nCharacteristic specific acoustic impedance (z₀) of medium\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.impedance"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.internal_field-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, Particle{Dim, Acoustic{T, Dim}}, AbstractSource{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, T, AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 1}}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"internal_field(x::AbstractVector, p::Particle{Dim,Acoustic{T,Dim}},  source::AbstractSource, ω::T, scattering_coefficients::AbstractVector{Complex{T}})\n\nThe internal field for an acoustic particle in an acoustic medium. For a sphere and circular cylinder the result is exact, for everything else it is an approximation which assumes smooth fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.internal_field"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.pressure_release-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"pressure_release(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_soft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.pressure_release"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.rigid-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"rigid(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_hard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.rigid"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.soft-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"soft(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_soft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.soft"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.sound_hard-Tuple{Type, Integer}","page":"Acoustic","text":"sound_hard([T::Type = Float64,] Dim::Integer)\n\nConstruct physical properties of a sound hard acoustic object with type T and dimension Dim. Also known as rigid and equivalent to a zero_neumann pressure boundary condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.sound_hard"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.sound_soft-Tuple{Type, Integer}","page":"Acoustic","text":"sound_soft([T::Type = Float64,] Dim::Integer)\n\nConstruct physical properties of a sound soft acoustic object with type T and dimension Dim. Equivalent to a zero_dirichlet pressure boundary condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.sound_soft"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.zero_dirichlet-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"zero_dirichlet(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_soft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.zero_dirichlet"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.zero_neumann-Union{Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Dim}} where {Dim, T}","page":"Acoustic","text":"zero_neumann(host_medium::Acoustic)\n\nSee sound_hard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.zero_neumann"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Particle{2, Acoustic{T, 2}, Sphere{T, 2}}, Acoustic{T, 2}, T, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"t_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Sphere{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D circlular acoustic particle in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Particle{3, Acoustic{T, 3}, Sphere{T, 3}}, Acoustic{T, 3}, T, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"t_matrix(Particle{3,Acoustic{T,3},Sphere{T,3}}, Acoustic{T,3}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 3D spherical acoustic particle in a 3D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CapsuleParticle{2, Acoustic{T, 2}, Sphere{T, 2}}, Acoustic{T, 2}, T, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"t_matrix(CapsuleParticle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Sphere{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D circlular capsule particle in an acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Particle{2, Acoustic{T, 2}, Helmholtz{T, 2}}, Acoustic{T, 2}, T, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"t_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},Helmholtz{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D non-isotropic Helmholtz resonator in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.t_matrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Particle{2, Acoustic{T, 2}, IsotropicHelmholtz{T, 2}}, Acoustic{T, 2}, T, Integer}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"t_matrix(Particle{2,Acoustic{T,2},IsotropicHelmholtz{T,2}}, Acoustic{T,2}, ω, order)\n\nThe T-matrix for a 2D isotropic Helmholtz resonator in a 2D acoustic medium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.t_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.plane_source-Union{Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}, AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}, AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{Acoustic{T, Dim}, AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}, Union{Complex{T}, Function, T}}} where {T, Dim}","page":"Acoustic","text":"plane_source(medium::Acoustic, position, direction = azimuthalnormal(Dim), amplitude = 1; causal = false, beam_width = Inf)::RegularSource\n\nCreate an Acoustic planar wave RegularSource. The default direction is [1,0] in 2D and [0,0,1] in 3D. The amplitude can be a number or a function of the angular frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.plane_source"},{"category":"method","location":"library/acoustics/#MultipleScattering.point_source-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Acoustic{T, 2}, AbstractVector}, Tuple{Acoustic{T, 2}, AbstractVector, Union{Complex{T}, Function, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Acoustic","text":"point_source(medium::Acoustic, source_position, amplitude=1)::RegularSource\n\nCreate 2D Acoustic point RegularSource (zeroth Hankel function of first type)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.point_source"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/source/#RegularSources","page":"RegularSources","text":"RegularSource is a struct which represents any source, also called an incident wave. See RegularSource for a list of relevant types and functions.","title":"RegularSources"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/source/#Acoustics","page":"RegularSources","text":"For acoustics, any wave field u_textin(mathbf x) that satisfies nabla^2 u_textin(mathbf x) + k^2 u_textin(mathbf x) = 0, with k = dfracomegac, can be a source.\n\nTwo commonly used sources are a plane wave and a point source. These can then be added together to create more complicated sources, like imitating a finite sized transducer / source.\n\nFor a plane-wave of the form u_textin(mathbf x) = A mathrm e^mathrm i k mathbf n cdot (mathbf x - mathbf x_0), where A is the amplitude, mathbf n = (n_1n_2n_3) is a unit vector which points in the direction of propagation, and mathbf x_0 = (x_0y_0z_0) is the initial position (or origin) of the source, we can use\n\njulia> using MultipleScattering;\n\njulia> dimension = 3;\n\njulia> medium = Acoustic(dimension; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0);\n\njulia> A = 1.0;\n\njulia> n = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0];\n\njulia> x0 = [1.0, 0.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> plane_wave = plane_source(medium; amplitude = A, direction = n, position = x0);\n\n\nThe direction is normalised to a unit vector. The amplitude A can also be a function of the angular frequency ω, for example amplitude = ω -> exp(-ω^2). With the keyword causal = true the plane wave is zero behind the plane mathbf n cdot (mathbf x - mathbf x_0) = 0, and with beam_width = w it is zero at a distance greater than w/2 from the line which passes through mathbf x_0 in the direction mathbf n.\n\nWe can plot this source wave one frequency ω by using\n\njulia> ω = 1.0;\n\njulia> plot_origin = zeros(3); plot_dimensions = 2 .* ones(3);\n\njulia> plot_domain = Box(plot_origin, plot_dimensions);\n\njulia> using Plots;\n\njulia> plot(plane_wave, ω; region_shape = plot_domain, y = 0.0) # in 3d currently only x-z slices are plotted for a given fixed y\n\n(Image: Plot plane wave)\n\nAnother useful source is the point source. In any dimension we consider the point source to be the zero order of the outgoing_basis_function, which are the basis for all outgoing waves.\n\nThe point source for 2D is u_textin(mathbf x) = fracmathrm i A4 mathrm H_0^(1)(k mathbf x - mathbf x_0) and for 3D it is\n\nu_textin(mathbf x) = A  mathrm h_0^(1)(k mathbf x - mathbf x_0) mathrm Y_0^0 = - fracmathrm i Asqrt4 pi fracmathrm e^mathrm i k  mathbf x - mathbf x_0k  mathbf x - mathbf x_0\n\nwhere A is the amplitude, mathbf x_0 is the origin of the point source, mathrm H_0^(1) is the Hankel function of the first kind, mathrm h_0^(1) is the spherical Hankel function of the first kind, and mathrm Y_0^0 = 1  sqrt4 pi is a spherical harmonic.\n\njulia> x0 = [0.0,-1.2, 0.0];\n\njulia> point_wave = point_source(medium, x0, A);\n\n\njulia> plot(point_wave, ω; region_shape = plot_domain)\n\n(Image: Plot point wave)\n\nnote: Note\nBecause the point source has a singularity at mathbf x_0 it is best to avoid plotting, and evaluating the field, close to mathbf x_0. This can be achieved by using run(point_wave, plot_domain, [ω]; exclude_region = some_region) or plot(point_wave, ω; region_shape = plot_domain, exclude_region = some_region), both of which rely on the function points_in_shape.","title":"Acoustics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/source/#Creating-new-sources","page":"RegularSources","text":"The easiest way to create new sources is to just sum together predefined sources:\n\njulia> source = (3.0 + 1.0im) * point_wave + plane_wave;\n\njulia> plot(source, ω; bounds = plot_domain)\n\n(Image: Plot combined source wave)\n\nFor example, we can use this to create a finite emitter/transducer source,\n\njulia> xs = LinRange(-0.7, 0.7, 30);\n\njulia> source = sum(xs) do x point_source(medium, [x, 0.0, -1.1]) end;\n\n\njulia> plot(source, 4.0; y = 0.0, bounds = plot_domain, field_apply = abs, resolution = 40)\n\n(Image: Plot point wave)\n\nwhere field_apply is applied to the wave field at every point, the default is field_apply = real, and resolution is the resolution along both the x and y axis. Note, this is not computationally very efficient. See source.jl for the very abstract code behind the scenes.\n\nA source can also be defined from its coefficients in the regular basis, explained below, by using regular_spherical_source.\n\nTo define a new source you will need to understand the internals below.","title":"Creating new sources"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/source/#RegularSource-internals","page":"RegularSources","text":"The struct RegularSource has three fields: medium, field, and coefficients, explained with examples below:\n\njulia> plane_wave = plane_source(Acoustic(1.0, 1.0, 2); direction = [1.0,0.0]);\n\njulia> plane_wave.medium # the physical medium\nAcoustic(1.0, 1.0 + 0.0im, 2)\n\njulia> x = [1.0, 1.0]; ω = 1.0;\n\njulia> plane_wave.field(x,ω) # the value of the field\n0.5403023058681398 + 0.8414709848078965im\n\nTo calculate the scattering from a particle due to a source, we need to represent the source in a radial coordinate system. That is, for any origin mathbf x_0, we need to represent the incident wave u_textin(mathbf x) using a series of regular waves\n\nu_textin(mathbf x) = sum_n a_n (mathbf x_0 omega) mathrm v_n (mathbf x - mathbf x_0)\n\nwhere for the scalar wave equation:\n\nmathrm v_n (mathbf x) = begincases\n   mathrm J_n(k r) mathrm e^mathrm i theta n  textfor  mathbf x in mathbb R^2 \n   mathrm j_ell(k r) mathrm Y_ell^m (hatmathbf x)  textfor  mathbf x in mathbb R^3 \nendcases\n\nwhere for the first case (rtheta) are the polar coordinates and n sums over -N-N+1 cdots N, where N is the basis order, and mathrm J_n is a Bessel function. For the second case, we use spherical coordinates with r =  mathbf x and hatmathbf x = mathbf xmathbf x, n denotes the multi-index n=ellm with summation over ell = 0 1 cdotsN and m=-ell-ell+1cdotsell, mathrm j_ell is a spherical Bessel function, and mathrm Y_ell^m is a spherical harmonic.\n\nThe vector of all the coefficients a_n(mathbf x_0 omega), up to the basis order N, is given by source.coefficients(N, x0, ω). The function regular_basis_function returns the row vector of all the mathrm v_n(mathbf x - mathbf x_0), so the series above is a matrix product. For example, for the inbuilt plane wave:\n\njulia> medium = Acoustic(2.0, 0.1, 2);\n\njulia> source = plane_source(medium);\n\njulia> x0 = [1.0, 1.0]; ω = 0.9;\n\njulia> x = x0 + [0.05, -0.03]; basis_order = 10;\n\njulia> vs = regular_basis_function(medium, ω);\n\njulia> source.field(x, ω) ≈ (vs(basis_order, x - x0) * source.coefficients(basis_order, x0, ω))[1]\ntrue\n\nThe package also supplies a convenience function source_expand, which represents the source in terms of regular waves, for example:\n\njulia> source2 = source_expand(source, x0; basis_order = 10);\n\njulia> source.field(x, ω) ≈ source2(x, ω)\ntrue\n\nTo create a new source, you need to provide both functions field(x, ω) and coefficients(N, x0, ω) to the constructor RegularSource(medium, field, coefficients).","title":"RegularSource internals"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#Introduction","page":"Introduction","text":"","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#into_physical_properties","page":"Introduction","text":"First define the host medium, for example for an acoustic medium in 2D\n\njulia> using MultipleScattering;\n\njulia> spatial_dim = 2; # could also be 3, but then all 2D vectors below would need to be 3D\n\njulia> host_medium = Acoustic(spatial_dim; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0) # density ρ = 1.0 and soundspeed c = 1.0\nAcoustic(1.0, 1.0 + 0.0im, 2)\n\nAt this step we have restricted the physics to acoustics, that is, solutions to the Helmholtz equation: nabla^2 u(xyomega) + k^2 u(xyomega) = 0, where k = dfracomegac, omega is the angular frequency and c the sound speed of the medium.","title":"Physical properties wave"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#into_source","page":"Introduction","text":"The host medium will determine the types of waves that can propagate. For example an incident plane wave mathrm e^ mathrm i k x there is a convenient constructor\n\njulia> source = plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0, 0.0]);\n\n\nnote: Note\nOften mathrm e^ mathrm i k x - mathrm i omega t is considered to be a harmonic plane-wave travelling along the x-axis. We omit the part $ - \\mathrm i \\omega t$ as is common in frequency space.\n\nWe generally call the incident wave a source. See RegularSources for details, and see Acoustic for some functions for the Acoustic medium.","title":"RegularSource wave"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#into_particles","page":"Introduction","text":"Next, we define some particles to scatter an acoustic wave. We choose two filled circles, the first centred at [-2, 2] with radius 2 and the second at [-2, -2] with radius 0.5,\n\njulia> particle_medium =  Acoustic(spatial_dim; ρ = 10.0, c = 2.0); # 2D acoustic particle with density ρ = 10.0 and soundspeed c = 2.0\n\njulia> p1 = Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([-2.0, 2.0], 2.0));\n\njulia> p2 = Particle(particle_medium, Sphere([-2.0, -2.0], 0.5));\n\njulia> particles = [p1, p2];\n\n\nSee Shapes and Particles for details on different shapes and particles.\n\nIf you have the package Plots installed you can plot the particles. Note that although they appear hollow, we consider them to be filled with the same homogeneous material.\n\njulia> using Plots;\n\njulia> plot(particles)\n\nnote: Note\nMost things in this package can be plotted just by typing plot(thing), as long as you have the package Plots installed. See Plotting for details on plotting.\n\n(Image: Plot of response against wavenumber)","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#Running-a-simulation","page":"Introduction","text":"Once we have the medium, the particles, and the source, we can build a FrequencySimulation object\n\njulia> simulation = FrequencySimulation(particles, source);\n\n\nTo get numerical results, we run our simulation for specific positions and angular frequencies,\n\njulia> x = [[-10.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> max_ω = 1.0;\n\njulia> ωs = 0.01:0.01:max_ω;\n\njulia> result = run(simulation, x, ωs);\n\n\nThe accuracy of the result depends on the keyword basis_order of run, which has the default basis_order = 5. See Simulations and results for what run calculates, how to choose basis_order, and how to use result.\n\nWe can plot the time-harmonic response across the frequencies ωs wavenumbers and at the location (-10,0) by typing:\n\njulia> plot(result)\n\n(Image: Plot of response against wavenumber)\n\nFor a better overview you can calculate the response for lots of points x in the domain and then plot the whole field for one frequency ω by typing:\n\njulia> ω = 0.8;\n\njulia> plot(simulation, ω);\n\njulia> plot!(particles)\n\n(Image: Plot real part of acoustic field)\n\nFor details on plot fields and videos see Plotting.","title":"Running a simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/intro/#Results-in-time","page":"Introduction","text":"If we have calculated a frequency response hat u(omega) over a range of frequencies omega, then we can use a Fourier transform to calculate the response in time u(t). That is, we can calculate u(t) by approximating the Fourier transform:\n\nu(t) = frac12pi int_-infty^infty hat u(omega)mathrm e^-mathrm i omega t domega\n\nFor details see the section on Time response. For example, taking a Discrete Fourier transform of the previous response leads to an incident plane wave pulse in time:\n\njulia> time_result = frequency_to_time(result);\n\njulia> plot(time_result)\n\n(Image: Plot real part of acoustic field)\n\nIn the image above the first peak on the left is due to the incident wave (the source), and the second peak is the wave scattered by the particles. Note how both peaks are quite jagged. This is due to Gibbs phenomena. To resolve this we can use a Gaussian impulse function shown below. See Time response for more details.\n\njulia> t_vec = LinRange(0.,700.,400);\n\njulia> gauss_time_result = frequency_to_time(result; t_vec = t_vec, impulse = GaussianImpulse(max_ω));\n\njulia> plot(gauss_time_result)\n\n(Image: Plot real part of acoustic field)","title":"Results in time"},{"category":"section","location":"library/shapes/#base_shapes","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Shapes are used to define the shape of particles, and to define containers (or configurations) where all particles are placed. It is also used in plotting.","title":"Shapes and domains"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Shape","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Abstract idea which defines the external boundary of object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Shape"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Shape-Tuple{Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Shape(shape::Shape; addtodimensions = 0.0, vector_translation = zeros(...), kws...)\n\nAlter the shape depending on the keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Shape"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#Base.in-Tuple{AbstractVector, Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"in(vector, shape)::Bool\n\nReturns true if vector is in interior of shape, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#Base.issubset-Tuple{Shape, Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"issubset(shape1, shape2)::Bool\n\nReturns true if shape1 is entirely contained within shape2, false otherwise (also works with particles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.:≅-Tuple{Shape, Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"iscongruent(p1::Shape, p2::Shape)::Bool\n≅(p1::Shape, p2::Shape)::Bool\n\nTrue if shapes are the same but in different positions (origins), standard mathematical definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.:≅"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.boundary_functions","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"boundary_functions(shape::Shape)::NTuple{Dim,Function}\n\nReturns Tuple of Dim Functions which define outer boundary of shape when given boundary coordinate t∈[0,1]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.boundary_functions"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.boundary_points","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Returns a set of points on the boundary of a 2D shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.boundary_points"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.boundary_points-2","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Returns a set of points on the boundary of a 3D shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.boundary_points"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.bounding_box-Tuple{Shape, Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Returns box which completely encloses the shapes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.bounding_box"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.check_boundary_coord_range-Tuple{Any}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Generic helper function which tests if boundary coordinate is between 0 and 1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.check_boundary_coord_range"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.congruent","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"congruent(s::Shape, x)::Shape\n\nCreate shape congruent to s but with origin at x\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.congruent"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.iscongruent-Tuple{Shape, Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"iscongruent(p1::Shape, p2::Shape)::Bool\n≅(p1::Shape, p2::Shape)::Bool\n\nTrue if shapes are the same but in different positions (origins), standard mathematical definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.iscongruent"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.name","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"name(shape::Shape)::String\n\nName of a shape\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.name"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.number_type-Tuple{Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"number_type(shape::Shape)::DataType\n\nNumber type which is used to describe shape, defaults to the eltype of the origin vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.number_type"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.origin-Tuple{Shape}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"origin(shape::Shape)::SVector\n\nOrigin of shape, typically the center\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.origin"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.outer_radius","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"outer_radius(shape::Shape)\n\nThe radius of a circle which completely contains the shape\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.outer_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.points_in_shape-Tuple{Shape{2}}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"points_in_shape(Shape; resolution = 20, xres = resolution, yres = resolution,\n         exclude_region = EmptyShape(region), kws...)\n\nreturns (x_vec, region_inds) where x_vec is a vector of points that cover a box which bounds Shape, and region_inds is an array of linear indices such that x_vec[region_inds] are points contained Shape. For 3D we use zres instead of yres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.points_in_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.volume","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"volume(shape::Shape)\n\nVolume of a shape\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.volume"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.AbstractSphere","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"All abstract spheres will have an origin and a radius \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.AbstractSphere"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Helmholtz","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Helmholtz([origin=zeros(),] radius, aperture)\n\nA Shape which represents a general 2D Helmholtz resonator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Helmholtz"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.IsotropicHelmholtz","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"IsotropicHelmholtz([origin=zeros(),] radius, aperture)\n\nA Shape which represents a 2D thin-walled isotropic Helmholtz resonator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.IsotropicHelmholtz"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Sphere","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Sphere([origin=zeros(),] radius)\n\nA Shape where boundary is a fixed distance from the origin. In 2D this is a circle, in 3D the usual sphere, and in higher dimensions if difficult to visualise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Sphere"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Halfspace","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Halfspace(normal::AbstractVector{T} [, origin::AbstractVector{T}=zeros()])\n\nA halfspace defined by all the points mathbf x that satify (mathbf x - mathbf o) cdot mathbf n  0 where mathbf n is the unit normal and mathbf o is the origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Halfspace"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Plate","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Plate(normal::AbstractVector{T}, width::T, [, origin::AbstractVector{T}=zeros()])\n\nA plate defined by all the points mathbf x that satify (mathbf x - mathbf o) cdot mathbf n  w 2 where mathbf n is the unit normal, mathbf o is the origin, and w is the width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Plate"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Box","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Box([origin::AbstractVector{T}=zeros(),] dimensions::AbstractVector{T})\n\nA Box for 2D and 3D with axis aligned sides, defined by dimensions and origin (at the center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Box"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Box-Tuple{Vector{v} where v<:(AbstractVector)}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Box(points::Vector{v} where v <: AbstractVector)\n\nA Box for any dimension with axis aligned sides, that is a minimal cover for the points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Box"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.bottomleft-Union{Tuple{Box{T, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Return SVector with the coordinates of the bottom left of a rectangle\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.bottomleft"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.corners-Union{Tuple{Box{T, Dim}}, Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{T}} where {T, Dim}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Returns a vector of SVector with the coordinates of the corners of the box\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.corners"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.topright-Union{Tuple{Box{T, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Return SVector with the coordinates of the top right of a rectangle\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.topright"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.EmptyShape","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"EmptyShape{Dim}\n\nAn empty shape with no points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.EmptyShape"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.Polygon","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"Polygon(boundary_points::Vector; interior_points = [mean(boundary_points)])\n\nA Shape defined by a set of points on the boundary boundary_points. The boundary_points should be ordered such that consecutive points are connected by edges of the polygon. The interior_points are a set of points that lie within the polygon. To determine whether a point is inside the polygon, see in(x::AbstractVector, poly::Polygon).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.Polygon"},{"category":"method","location":"library/shapes/#Base.issubset-Tuple{Box, Polygon}","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"issubset(box::Box, poly::Polygon)\n\nReturns true if the corners of the box are contained within polygon, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"A shape which contains all particles,x  x_0, necessary to simulate a plane-wave scattering from an infinite medium, for a reciever at the focal point, for time t  D  c, where c is the sound speed of the background medium, and D is some chosen focal distance. The plane-wave travels towards the positive direction of the x axis.\n\nMore precisely, if the focal point is at (x_fy_f) then the interior of the shape is defined as (y - y_f)^2  (D + x_0)^2 - x_f^2 - 2(D + x_0 - x_f)x  and x  min(x_0 x_f) where D is the focal distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.TimeOfFlightPlaneWaveToPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"library/shapes/#MultipleScattering.TimeOfFlightPointWaveToPoint","page":"Shapes and domains","text":"A shape where anything inside could cause a disturbance at the listener position from a point source wavefront starting at the listener. Also everything inside has a positive x coordinate. It is equivalent to a segment of a circle.\n\nMore precisely, if the listener is at (lx,ly) then the interior of the shape is defined as sqrt((x-lx)^2+(y-ly)^2)<time and x>0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.TimeOfFlightPointWaveToPoint"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#Overview","page":"Overview","text":"Each example below has its own folder in docs/src/example with the full code. Most examples need the package Plots for the figures.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Helmholtz-resonator](helmholtz-resonator/resonator.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Build a Helmholtz resonator from small particles placed in the shape of a bottle.\nPlot the field for different frequencies to find the resonant frequencies, where the field inside the cavity is large.","title":"Helmholtz resonator"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Simple-random-particles](random_particles/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Place random particles in a rectangle, calculate the response at a listener for many frequencies, and plot the field.","title":"Simple random particles"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Random-particles-in-a-circle](particles_in_circle/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Compare the wave scattered by one big circle with the wave scattered by a circle filled with small particles.","title":"Random particles in a circle"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Box-size](box_size/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Find which particles need to be simulated to calculate the response at a listener for a given time interval.","title":"Box size"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Convergence-of-the-basis-order](hankel_convergence/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Check how fast the scattered wave converges when increasing the number of Hankel functions, that is, the basis order.","title":"Convergence of the basis order"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Statistical-moments](moments/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Extract statistical information from many simulations with random particles: the mean, standard deviation, skew and kurtosis (also known as moments).","title":"Statistical moments"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Near-surface-backscattering](near_surface_backscattering/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"A method to calculate the backscattering from an infinite halfspace filled with particles.\nSee backscattering in frequency and time.","title":"Near surface backscattering"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Time-response-from-a-single-particle](time_response_single_particle/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Calculate the response in time from a plane wave pulse which is reflected by one particle.","title":"Time response from a single particle"},{"category":"section","location":"example/README/#[Two-particles](two_particles/README.md)","page":"Overview","text":"Calculate and plot the field scattered by two particles with different properties.","title":"Two particles"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/new_types/#New-particles","page":"New particles","text":"If you are feeling very adventurous, you can define a new type of particle.\n\nA particle is defined by its shape and physical properties. The simplest example being a circular acoustics particle: Particle(Acoustic(1.0,1.0,2),Circle(1.0)). To define a new particle you need to either use a new shape or a new physical medium.","title":"New particles"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/new_types/#New-shape","page":"New particles","text":"Suppose we want to define a new shape, which is very much like a rectangle.\n\nusing MultipleScattering\n\nstruct MyRectangle{T} <: Shape{2}\n    origin::Vector{T}\n    width::T\n    height::T\nend\n\nTo be able to place particles in MyRectangle you need to extend the functions defined in the package you need to explicitly import them:\n\nimport MultipleScattering: volume, name, bounding_box\n\nvolume(shape::MyRectangle) = shape.width * shape.height\nname(shape::MyRectangle) = \"MyRectangle\"\n\n# every shape needs a bounding rectangle, this is where particles are first placed.\nbounding_box(shape::MyRectangle) = Box(shape.origin, [shape.width, shape.height])\n\nimport Base.in, Base.issubset\nfunction in(x::AbstractVector, r::MyRectangle)::Bool\n    all(abs.(x .- r.origin) .<= [r.width, r.height] ./ 2)\nend\n\nfunction issubset(circle::Sphere{T,2}, r::MyRectangle{T}) where {T}\n    all((origin(circle) .- circle.radius) .>= r.origin - [r.width/2.0, r.height/2.0]) &&\n    all((origin(circle) .+ circle.radius) .<= r.origin + [r.width/2.0, r.height/2.0])\nend\n\nnote: Note\nUsing other packages with MultipleScattering may result in a naming conflict for functions like volume. In this case you will have to explicitly call MultipleScattering.volume.\n\nWith these definitions you can now fill this shape with circular particles, as shown in Placing particles in a region.\n\nTo define a new particle from MyRectangle you need to extend the functions outer_radius, Base.(==), iscongruent, then supposing you will use a predefined PhysicalMedium (such as Acoustic), you then need to extend t_matrix.","title":"New shape"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/new_types/#New-T-matrix","page":"New particles","text":"The scattering from a particle is completely determined by its T-matrix. To simulate a new type of particle, you need to extend t_matrix for that particle and the background medium. The T-matrix maps the coefficients of the wave incident on the particle, in the regular basis, to the coefficients of the scattered wave, in the outgoing basis. See What run calculates for the conventions, and the Notes on the T-matrix.\n\nFor example, in 2D a circular particle where the field is zero on the boundary (a sound-soft particle), with radius a, has the diagonal T-matrix with entries -mathrm J_n(k a)  mathrm H^(1)_n(k a), for n = -N ldots N, where N is the basis order.","title":"New T-matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/new_types/#New-physical-medium","page":"New particles","text":"A physical medium is a subtype of PhysicalMedium{Dim,FieldDim}, where Dim is the spatial dimension and FieldDim is the dimension of the field, for example FieldDim = 1 for a scalar field.\n\nFor a scalar wave which satisfies a Helmholtz equation, the medium only needs a field c, the wave speed. Then the basis functions and translation matrices of the package are used. You need to define the T-matrix of the particles, and a source. For example, the code below defines a new medium and sound-soft circular particles in that medium:\n\nusing MultipleScattering, LinearAlgebra, SpecialFunctions\nimport MultipleScattering: t_matrix\n\nstruct MyMedium{T,Dim} <: PhysicalMedium{Dim,1}\n    c::Complex{T} # the wave speed\nend\n\nfunction t_matrix(p::Particle{2,MyMedium{T,2},Sphere{T,2}}, medium::MyMedium{T,2}, ω::T, basis_order::Integer) where T\n    ka = ω / medium.c * outer_radius(p)\n    return Diagonal([- besselj(n, ka) / hankelh1(n, ka) for n = -basis_order:basis_order])\nend\n\nmedium = MyMedium{Float64,2}(1.0)\n\n# a source given by its coefficients in the regular basis, here the wave J_0(k r)\nsource = regular_spherical_source(medium, [1.0 + 0.0im])\n\nparticles = [Particle(medium, Sphere([2.0, 0.0], 0.5)), Particle(medium, Sphere([-1.0, 1.0], 0.5))]\n\nresult = run(particles, source, [[4.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]], 1.0)\n\nThis gives the same result as using acoustic particles with the medium sound_soft(2) in the medium Acoustic(2; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0). To also calculate the field inside the particles, you need to extend internal_field.\n\nFor waves with more than one component, such as elastic waves, you also need to extend the functions which define the basis:\n\nbasisorder_to_basislength and basislength_to_basisorder, the number of basis functions for each basis order;\nregular_basis_function and outgoing_basis_function;\nregular_translation_matrix and outgoing_translation_matrix.\n\nSee the package ElasticWaves.jl for an example.","title":"New physical medium"},{"category":"section","location":"#MultipleScattering.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia library for simulating, processing, and plotting multiple scattering of waves.\n\nMultipleScattering.jl calculates how time-harmonic waves are scattered by many particles. It uses the multipole method, also called the T-matrix method, to solve the Helmholtz equation. This method is particularly efficient for particles in an infinite domain. Each particle is represented by its T-matrix, so particles can have any shape and properties for which a T-matrix is known.\n\nThe package is set up for different spatial dimensions and different types of waves which satisfy Helmholtz equations, such as acoustics, electromagnetism, and elasticity. Acoustics in 2D and 3D is implemented here. For details on the maths see Martin (1995), Gower et al. (2017), and the notes in Theory.","title":"MultipleScattering.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Acoustic waves in two and three spatial dimensions.\nParticles: penetrable, sound-hard and sound-soft circles and spheres, concentric capsules, and 2D Helmholtz resonators. New particles only need a T-matrix, see New particles.\nSources (incident waves): plane waves, point sources, beams, and any linear combination of these. See RegularSources.\nResults in the frequency domain, and in the time domain by using Fourier transforms with smooth impulses. See Time response.\nRandom placement of particles in a region, for a given volume fraction, and statistics over many random configurations.\nPlot recipes for particles, shapes, sources, fields, and results. See Plotting.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Install Julia v1.10 or later, then run\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"MultipleScattering\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-example","page":"Home","text":"Calculate the field scattered by a single circular particle when it is hit by a plane wave:\n\nusing MultipleScattering\n\nhost_medium = Acoustic(2; ρ = 1.0, c = 1.0)  # 2D acoustics, density 1.0 and sound speed 1.0\nparticle = Particle(Acoustic(2; ρ = 0.5, c = 0.6), Sphere([0.0, 0.0], 1.0))\nsource = plane_source(host_medium; direction = [1.0, 0.0])\n\n# the total field at the point x = [3.0, 0.0] for the angular frequency ω = 2.0\nresult = run([particle], source, [3.0, 0.0], 2.0)\nround.(field(result), digits = 6)\n\n# output\n\n1×1 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n 0.391303 + 1.054422im\n\nIf you have the package Plots installed, then plot(FrequencySimulation([particle], source), 2.0) plots the whole field around the particle.","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"The Introduction walks through a complete simulation, from the medium to plotting results in time.\nSimulations and results explains what run calculates, how to choose basis_order, and how to use the results.\nThe manual pages on RegularSources, Shapes, and Particles describe the building blocks in more detail.\nThe Examples show complete scripts, many with plots.\nThe Library lists every type and function.","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-packages","page":"Home","text":"These packages build on MultipleScattering.jl:\n\nEffectiveWaves.jl: ensemble averaged (effective) waves in materials filled with random particles.\nElasticWaves.jl: elastic waves, including scattering from elastic particles.\nParticleCorrelations.jl: pair correlations and structure factors of particle configurations.\nMethodOfFundamentalSolutions.jl: the method of fundamental solutions for wave and elastostatic problems.","title":"Related packages"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\n    \"manual/intro.md\",\n    \"manual/simulation.md\",\n    \"manual/source.md\",\n    \"manual/shapes.md\",\n    \"manual/particles.md\",\n    \"manual/time_response.md\",\n    \"manual/plot.md\",\n    \"manual/new_types.md\",\n]\nDepth = 1","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"library/random/#Random","page":"Random","text":"","title":"Random"},{"category":"section","location":"library/random/#Random-particles","page":"Random","text":"","title":"Random particles"},{"category":"section","location":"library/random/#Moments","page":"Random","text":"","title":"Moments"},{"category":"function","location":"library/random/#MultipleScattering.random_particles","page":"Random","text":"random_particles(particle_medium, particle_shapes::Vector{Shape}, region_shape, volume_fraction::Number;\n    seed=Random.make_seed())\nrandom_particles(particle_medium, particle_shape::Shape, region_shape, volume_fraction::Number;\n    seed=Random.make_seed())\nrandom_particles(particle_medium, particle_shape::Shape, region_shape, N::Integer;\n    seed=Random.make_seed())\n\nGenerate N random particles that fit inside region_shape (or fill with volume_fraction)\n\nSpecify seed to make output deterministic. Algorithm places particles unifomly randomly inside the bounding box of region_shape and discards particle if it overlaps (based on outer radius) or does not lies completely in box.\n\nWhen passing particle_shapes::Vector{Shape} we assume each element is equally likely to occur. Repeating the same shape will lead to it being placed more often.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.random_particles"},{"category":"function","location":"library/random/#MultipleScattering.statistical_moments","page":"Random","text":"statistical_moments(results, n; field_apply=real)::Vector{Matrix}\n\nCalculate moments up to n of results at each position and wavenumber/time, after applying field_apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultipleScattering.statistical_moments"}]
}
